<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Goals">Goals</h2></div>
<p>The general problem of simulating (or creating) intelligence has been br=
oken into subproblems. These consist of particular traits or capabilities t=
hat researchers expect an intelligent system to display. The traits describ=
ed below have received the most attention and cover the scope of AI researc=
h.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Problems_of_AI_4-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Problems_of_A=
I-4"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>a<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Reasoning_and_problem-solvi=
ng">Reasoning and problem-solving</h3></div>
<p>Early researchers developed algorithms that imitated step-by-step reason=
ing that humans use when they solve puzzles or make logical <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deductive_reasoning" title=3D"Deductive reasoning=
">deductions</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-15" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-15"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>13<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> By the late 1980s and 1990s, methods were developed for dealing with <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uncertainty" title=3D"Uncertainty">unc=
ertain</a> or incomplete information, employing concepts from <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Probability" title=3D"Probability">probability<=
/a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Economics" title=3D"Econom=
ics">economics</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-16" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-16"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>14<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up>
</p><p>Many of these <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithm" ti=
tle=3D"Algorithm">algorithms</a> are insufficient for solving large reasoni=
ng problems because they experience a "combinatorial explosion": They becom=
e exponentially slower as the problems grow.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Intractabil=
ity_and_efficiency_and_the_combinatorial_explosion_17-0" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_not=
e-Intractability_and_efficiency_and_the_combinatorial_explosion-17"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>15<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></=
sup> Even humans rarely use the step-by-step deduction that early AI resear=
ch could model. They solve most of their problems using fast, intuitive jud=
gments.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Psychological_evidence_of_the_prevalence_of_sub_=
18-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifici=
al_intelligence#cite_note-Psychological_evidence_of_the_prevalence_of_sub-1=
8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>16<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup> Accurate and efficient reasoning is an unsolved problem.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Knowledge_representation">K=
nowledge representation</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:General_Formal_Ontology.svg" class=3D"mw-file=
-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/th=
umb/e/e8/General_Formal_Ontology.svg/330px-General_Formal_Ontology.svg.png"=
 decoding=3D"async" width=3D"300" height=3D"300" class=3D"mw-file-element" =
data-file-width=3D"512" data-file-height=3D"512"></a><figcaption>An ontolog=
y represents knowledge as a set of concepts within a domain and the relatio=
nships between those concepts.</figcaption></figure>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knowledge_representation" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Knowledge representation">Knowledge representatio=
n</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knowledge_engineering" t=
itle=3D"Knowledge engineering">knowledge engineering</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref=
-19" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificia=
l_intelligence#cite_note-19"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>17<span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> allow AI programs to answer questi=
ons intelligently and make deductions about real-world facts. Formal knowle=
dge representations are used in content-based indexing and retrieval,<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESmoliarZhang1994_20-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTESm=
oliarZhang1994-20"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>18<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup> scene interpretation,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOO=
TNOTENeumannM=C3=B6ller2008_21-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTENeumannM%C3%B=
6ller2008-21"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>19<span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">]</span></a></sup> clinical decision support,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOO=
TNOTEKupermanReichleyBailey2006_22-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKupermanR=
eichleyBailey2006-22"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>20<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> knowledge discovery (mining "interesting"=
 and actionable inferences from large <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Database" title=3D"Database">databases</a>),<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOT=
EMcGarry2005_23-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcGarry2005-23"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>21<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=
 and other areas.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBertiniDel_BimboTorniai2006_24=
-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial=
_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBertiniDel_BimboTorniai2006-24"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>22<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>A <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knowledge_base" title=3D"=
Knowledge base">knowledge base</a> is a body of knowledge represented in a =
form that can be used by a program. An <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Ontology_(information_science)" title=3D"Ontology (information science=
)">ontology</a> is the set of objects, relations, concepts, and properties =
used by a particular domain of knowledge.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussel=
lNorvig2021272_25-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021272-25"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>23<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> Knowledge bases need to represent things such as objects, proper=
ties, categories, and relations between objects;<sup id=3D"cite_ref-26" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-26"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>24<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> situations, events, states, and time;<sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-27" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-27"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>25<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> causes and effects;<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-28" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-28"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>26<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> knowledge about =
knowledge (what we know about what other people know);<sup id=3D"cite_ref-2=
9" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_=
intelligence#cite_note-29"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>27<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Default_reasoning" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Default reasoning">d=
efault reasoning</a> (things that humans assume are true until they are tol=
d differently and will remain true even when other facts are changing);<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-Default_reasoning_30-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Default_reasoni=
ng-30"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>28<span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
]</span></a></sup> and many other aspects and domains of knowledge.
</p><p>Among the most difficult problems in knowledge representation are th=
e breadth of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Commonsense_knowledge=
" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Commonsense knowledge">commonsense knowled=
ge</a> (the set of atomic facts that the average person knows is enormous);=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Breadth_of_commonsense_knowledge_31-0" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_n=
ote-Breadth_of_commonsense_knowledge-31"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>29<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and the sub-symbolic f=
orm of most commonsense knowledge (much of what people know is not represen=
ted as "facts" or "statements" that they could express verbally).<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-Psychological_evidence_of_the_prevalence_of_sub_18-1" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#c=
ite_note-Psychological_evidence_of_the_prevalence_of_sub-18"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>16<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Th=
ere is also the difficulty of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Know=
ledge_acquisition" title=3D"Knowledge acquisition">knowledge acquisition</a=
>, the problem of obtaining knowledge for AI applications.<sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-34" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-34"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>c<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Planning_and_decision-makin=
g">Planning and decision-making</h3></div>
<p>An "agent" is any entity (artificial or not) that perceives and takes ac=
tions in the world. A <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rational_age=
nt" title=3D"Rational agent">rational agent</a> has goals or preferences an=
d takes actions to make them happen.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-35" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_=
note-35"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>d<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021528_36-0" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021528-36"><span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">[</span>32<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automated_planning" class=3D"mw-redirect"=
 title=3D"Automated planning">automated planning</a>, the agent has a speci=
fic goal.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-37" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-37"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>33<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automated_decision-making" title=3D"A=
utomated decision-making">automated decision-making</a>, the agent has pref=
erences=E2=80=94there are some situations it would prefer to be in, and som=
e situations it is trying to avoid. The decision-making agent assigns a num=
ber to each situation (called the "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Utility" title=3D"Utility">utility</a>") that measures how much the agent =
prefers it. For each possible action, it can calculate the "<a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expected_utility" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"=
Expected utility">expected utility</a>": the utility of all possible outcom=
es of the action, weighted by the probability that the outcome will occur. =
It can then choose the action with the maximum expected utility.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-38" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
rtificial_intelligence#cite_note-38"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3=
4<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automated_planning_and_s=
cheduling#classical_planning" title=3D"Automated planning and scheduling">c=
lassical planning</a>, the agent knows exactly what the effect of any actio=
n will be.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-39" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-39"><span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">[</span>35<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In mo=
st real-world problems, however, the agent may not be certain about the sit=
uation they are in (it is "unknown" or "unobservable") and it may not know =
for certain what will happen after each possible action (it is not "determi=
nistic"). It must choose an action by making a probabilistic guess and then=
 reassess the situation to see if the action worked.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-40"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_in=
telligence#cite_note-40"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>36<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=20
</p><p>Alongside thorough testing and improvement based on previous decisio=
ns, having an explanation for why the agent took certain decisions is a way=
 to build trust, especially when the decisions have to be relied upon.<sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-41" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-41"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>37<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In some problems, the agent's preferences may be uncertain, especial=
ly if there are other agents or humans involved. These can be learned (e.g.=
, with <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inverse_reinforcement_learn=
ing" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Inverse reinforcement learning">inverse=
 reinforcement learning</a>), or the agent can seek information to improve =
its preferences.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-42" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-42"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>38<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_value_theory" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Information value theory">Information value theor=
y</a> can be used to weigh the value of exploratory or experimental actions=
.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-43" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-43"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>39<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The space of p=
ossible future actions and situations is typically <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Intractably" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Intractably">i=
ntractably</a> large, so the agents must take actions and evaluate situatio=
ns while being uncertain of what the outcome will be.
</p><p>A <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markov_decision_process" =
title=3D"Markov decision process">Markov decision process</a> has a <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Finite-state_machine" title=3D"Finite-sta=
te machine">transition model</a> that describes the probability that a part=
icular action will change the state in a particular way and a reward functi=
on that supplies the utility of each state and the cost of each action. A <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinforcement_learning#Policy" titl=
e=3D"Reinforcement learning">policy</a> associates a decision with each pos=
sible state. The policy could be calculated (e.g., by <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Policy_iteration" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Policy=
 iteration">iteration</a>), be <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heu=
ristic" title=3D"Heuristic">heuristic</a>, or it can be learned.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-44" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
rtificial_intelligence#cite_note-44"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>4=
0<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_theory" title=3D"Game =
theory">Game theory</a> describes the rational behavior of multiple interac=
ting agents and is used in AI programs that make decisions that involve oth=
er agents.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-45" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-45"><span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">[</span>41<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Learning">Learning</h3></di=
v>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning" title=3D"Mach=
ine learning">Machine learning</a> is the study of programs that can improv=
e their performance on a given task automatically.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-46" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-46"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>42<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> It has been a part of AI from the begi=
nning.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-49" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-49"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>e<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Supervised_and_unsupervised_learning.png" cla=
ss=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikiped=
ia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Supervised_and_unsupervised_learning.png/500px-Superv=
ised_and_unsupervised_learning.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"350" height=
=3D"158" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"681" data-file-height=
=3D"308"></a><figcaption>In <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superv=
ised_learning" title=3D"Supervised learning">supervised learning</a>, the t=
raining data is labelled with the expected answers, while in <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unsupervised_learning" title=3D"Unsupervised lea=
rning">unsupervised learning</a>, the model identifies patterns or structur=
es in unlabelled data.</figcaption></figure>
<p>There are several kinds of machine learning. <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Unsupervised_learning" title=3D"Unsupervised learning">Unsupe=
rvised learning</a> analyzes a stream of data and finds patterns and makes =
predictions without any other guidance.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-50" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#ci=
te_note-50"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>45<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supervised=
_learning" title=3D"Supervised learning">Supervised learning</a> requires l=
abeling the training data with the expected answers, and comes in two main =
varieties: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Statistical_classificat=
ion" title=3D"Statistical classification">classification</a> (where the pro=
gram must learn to predict what category the input belongs in) and <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Regression_analysis" title=3D"Regression =
analysis">regression</a> (where the program must deduce a numeric function =
based on numeric input).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Supervised_learning_51-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-Supervised_learning-51"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>46<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinforcement_learning" =
title=3D"Reinforcement learning">reinforcement learning</a>, the agent is r=
ewarded for good responses and punished for bad ones. The agent learns to c=
hoose responses that are classified as "good".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-52" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-52"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>47<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tra=
nsfer_learning" title=3D"Transfer learning">Transfer learning</a> is when t=
he knowledge gained from one problem is applied to a new problem.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-53" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-53"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
48<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Deep_learning" title=3D"Deep learning">Deep learning</a> is=
 a type of machine learning that runs inputs through biologically inspired =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_neural_networks" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Artificial neural networks">artificial neural net=
works</a> for all of these types of learning.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-54" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-54"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>49<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_learning_theo=
ry" title=3D"Computational learning theory">Computational learning theory</=
a> can assess learners by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computat=
ional_complexity" title=3D"Computational complexity">computational complexi=
ty</a>, by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sample_complexity" titl=
e=3D"Sample complexity">sample complexity</a> (how much data is required), =
or by other notions of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optimizatio=
n" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Optimization">optimization</a>.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-55" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-55"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
50<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div style=3D"clear:both;" class=3D""></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Natural_language_processing=
">Natural language processing</h3></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_language_processing" ti=
tle=3D"Natural language processing">Natural language processing</a> (NLP) a=
llows programs to read, write and communicate in human languages.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-56" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-56"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
51<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Specific problems include=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speech_recognition" title=3D"Spee=
ch recognition">speech recognition</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Speech_synthesis" title=3D"Speech synthesis">speech synthesis</a>, <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_translation" title=3D"Machin=
e translation">machine translation</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Information_extraction" title=3D"Information extraction">information =
extraction</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_retrie=
val" title=3D"Information retrieval">information retrieval</a> and <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Question_answering" title=3D"Question ans=
wering">question answering</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-57" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-=
57"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>52<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup>
</p><p>Early work, based on <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noam_C=
homsky" title=3D"Noam Chomsky">Noam Chomsky</a>'s <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Generative_grammar" title=3D"Generative grammar">generative=
 grammar</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semantic_network"=
 title=3D"Semantic network">semantic networks</a>, had difficulty with <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word-sense_disambiguation" title=3D"Wo=
rd-sense disambiguation">word-sense disambiguation</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref-5=
8" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_=
intelligence#cite_note-58"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>f<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> unless restricted to small domains ca=
lled "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blocks_world" title=3D"Block=
s world">micro-worlds</a>" (due to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Commonsense_knowledge_(artificial_intelligence)" title=3D"Commonsense =
knowledge (artificial intelligence)">common sense knowledge problem</a><sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-Breadth_of_commonsense_knowledge_31-1" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-=
Breadth_of_commonsense_knowledge-31"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>2=
9<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>). <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Margaret_Masterman" title=3D"Margaret Masterman">Margaret =
Masterman</a> believed that it was meaning and not grammar that was the key=
 to understanding languages, and that <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Thesauri" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Thesauri">thesauri</a> and not=
 dictionaries should be the basis of computational language structure.
</p><p>Modern deep learning techniques for NLP include <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Word_embedding" title=3D"Word embedding">word embeddin=
g</a> (representing words, typically as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Vector_space" title=3D"Vector space">vectors</a> encoding their meani=
ng),<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021856=E2=80=93858_59-0" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021856%E2%80%93858-59"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>53<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transformer_(machine_learning_model)=
" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Transformer (machine learning model)">tran=
sformers</a> (a deep learning architecture using an <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Attention_(machine_learning)" title=3D"Attention (machine=
 learning)">attention</a> mechanism),<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDickson202=
2_60-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDickson2022-60"><span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">[</span>54<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and others.=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-61" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-61"><span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">[</span>55<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In 2019, <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generative_pre-trained_transformer" titl=
e=3D"Generative pre-trained transformer">generative pre-trained transformer=
</a> (or "GPT") language models began to generate coherent text,<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-FOOTNOTEVincent2019_62-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEVincent2019-=
62"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>56<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021875=E2=80=93878=
_63-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021875%E2%80%93878-63"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>57<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup> and by 2023, these models were able to get human-level scores on t=
he <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bar_exam" class=3D"mw-redirect"=
 title=3D"Bar exam">bar exam</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
SAT" title=3D"SAT">SAT</a> test, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G=
RE" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"GRE">GRE</a> test, and many other real-w=
orld applications.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEBushwick2023_64-0" class=3D"r=
eference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#=
cite_note-FOOTNOTEBushwick2023-64"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>58<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Perception">Perception</h3>=
</div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_perception" title=3D"Ma=
chine perception">Machine perception</a> is the ability to use input from s=
ensors (such as cameras, microphones, wireless signals, active <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lidar" title=3D"Lidar">lidar</a>, sonar, radar=
, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tactile_sensor" title=3D"Tac=
tile sensor">tactile sensors</a>) to deduce aspects of the world. <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_vision" title=3D"Computer vision=
">Computer vision</a> is the ability to analyze visual input.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-65" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arti=
ficial_intelligence#cite_note-65"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>59<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>The field includes <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speech_r=
ecognition" title=3D"Speech recognition">speech recognition</a>,<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021849=E2=80=93850_66-0" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021849%E2%80%93850-66"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>60<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image_classification" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"Image classification">image classification</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOT=
NOTERussellNorvig2021895=E2=80=93899_67-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERuss=
ellNorvig2021895%E2%80%93899-67"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>61<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Facial_recognition_system" title=3D"Facial recognition system">f=
acial recognition</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object_reco=
gnition" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Object recognition">object recognit=
ion</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021899=E2=80=93901_68-0" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_int=
elligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021899%E2%80%93901-68"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>62<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Motion_capture" title=3D"Motion =
capture">object tracking</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChallaMorelandMu=
=C5=A1ickiEvans2011_69-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChallaMorelandMu%C5%A=
1ickiEvans2011-69"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>63<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Robotic_perception" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Robotic perception">rob=
otic perception</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021931=E2=80=
=93938_70-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
rtificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021931%E2%80%93938-7=
0"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>64<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Social_intelligence">Social=
 intelligence</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Kismet-IMG_6007-gradient.jpg" class=3D"mw-fil=
e-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/t=
humb/2/27/Kismet-IMG_6007-gradient.jpg/250px-Kismet-IMG_6007-gradient.jpg" =
decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"167" class=3D"mw-file-element" d=
ata-file-width=3D"5500" data-file-height=3D"3667"></a><figcaption><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kismet_(robot)" title=3D"Kismet (robot)">=
Kismet</a>, a robot head which was made in the 1990s; it is a machine that =
can recognize and simulate emotions.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMIT_AIL2014=
_71-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMIT_AIL2014-71"><span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">[</span>65<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></figcaption>=
</figure>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affective_computing" title=3D"A=
ffective computing">Affective computing</a> is a field that comprises syste=
ms that recognize, interpret, process, or simulate human <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Affect_(psychology)" title=3D"Affect (psychology)">f=
eeling, emotion, and mood</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-72" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-7=
2"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>66<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup> For example, some <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V=
irtual_assistant" title=3D"Virtual assistant">virtual assistants</a> are pr=
ogrammed to speak conversationally or even to banter humorously; it makes t=
hem appear more sensitive to the emotional dynamics of human interaction, o=
r to otherwise facilitate <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human%E2=
%80%93computer_interaction" title=3D"Human=E2=80=93computer interaction">hu=
man=E2=80=93computer interaction</a>.
</p><p>However, this tends to give na=C3=AFve users an unrealistic concepti=
on of the intelligence of existing computer agents.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOT=
NOTEWaddell2018_73-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWaddell2018-73"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>67<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></su=
p> Moderate successes related to affective computing include textual <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentiment_analysis" title=3D"Sentiment a=
nalysis">sentiment analysis</a> and, more recently, <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Multimodal_sentiment_analysis" title=3D"Multimodal sentim=
ent analysis">multimodal sentiment analysis</a>, wherein AI classifies the =
effects displayed by a videotaped subject.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEPoria=
CambriaBajpaiHussain2017_74-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPoriaCambriaBajp=
aiHussain2017-74"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>68<span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"General_intelligence">Gener=
al intelligence</h3></div>
<p>A machine with <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_gener=
al_intelligence" title=3D"Artificial general intelligence">artificial gener=
al intelligence</a> would be able to solve a wide variety of problems with =
breadth and versatility similar to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Human_intelligence" title=3D"Human intelligence">human intelligence</a>.<s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-Artificial_general_intelligence_75-0" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note=
-Artificial_general_intelligence-75"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>6=
9<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Techniques">Techniques</h2>=
</div>
<p>AI research uses a wide variety of techniques to accomplish the goals ab=
ove.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Tools_of_AI_5-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Tools_of_AI-5"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>b<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span=
></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Search_and_optimization">Se=
arch and optimization</h3></div>
<p>There are two different kinds of search used in AI: <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_space_search" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Sta=
te space search">state space search</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Local_search_(optimization)" title=3D"Local search (optimization)=
">local search</a>:
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"State_space_search">State s=
pace search</h4></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/State_space_search" class=3D"mw=
-redirect" title=3D"State space search">State space search</a> searches thr=
ough a tree of possible states to try to find a goal state.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-76" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence#cite_note-76"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>70<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> For example, <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automated_planning_and_scheduling" title=3D"Automate=
d planning and scheduling">planning</a> algorithms search through trees of =
goals and subgoals, attempting to find a path to a target goal, a process c=
alled <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Means-ends_analysis" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Means-ends analysis">means-ends analysis</a>.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021sect._11.2_77-0" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_no=
te-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021sect._11.2-77"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>71<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brute_force_search" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Brute force search">Simple exhaustive searches</a=
><sup id=3D"cite_ref-78" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-78"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>72<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> are rarely suf=
ficient for most real-world problems: the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Search_algorithm" title=3D"Search algorithm">search space</a> (the =
number of places to search) quickly grows to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Astronomically_large" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Astronomica=
lly large">astronomical numbers</a>. The result is a search that is <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computation_time" class=3D"mw-redirect" t=
itle=3D"Computation time">too slow</a> or never completes.<sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-Intractability_and_efficiency_and_the_combinatorial_explosion_17-1" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-Intractability_and_efficiency_and_the_combinatorial_explosi=
on-17"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>15<span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
]</span></a></sup> "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heuristics" cl=
ass=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Heuristics">Heuristics</a>" or "rules of thumb=
" can help prioritize choices that are more likely to reach a goal.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-79" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-79"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>73<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adversarial_search" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Adversarial search">Adversarial search</a> is use=
d for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_AI" class=3D"mw-redirec=
t" title=3D"Game AI">game-playing</a> programs, such as chess or Go. It sea=
rches through a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Game_tree" title=
=3D"Game tree">tree</a> of possible moves and countermoves, looking for a w=
inning position.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-80" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-80"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>74<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Local_search">Local search<=
/h4></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Gradient_descent.gif" class=
=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia=
/commons/thumb/a/a3/Gradient_descent.gif/250px-Gradient_descent.gif" decodi=
ng=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"203" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-fi=
le-width=3D"360" data-file-height=3D"292"></a><figcaption>Illustration of <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradient_descent" title=3D"Gradient=
 descent">gradient descent</a> for 3 different starting points; two paramet=
ers (represented by the plan coordinates) are adjusted in order to minimize=
 the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loss_function" title=3D"Loss =
function">loss function</a> (the height)</figcaption></figure> <p><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Local_search_(optimization)" title=3D"Loc=
al search (optimization)">Local search</a> uses <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Mathematical_optimization" title=3D"Mathematical optimization=
">mathematical optimization</a> to find a solution to a problem. It begins =
with some form of guess and refines it incrementally.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-81=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_i=
ntelligence#cite_note-81"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>75<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradient_descent" title=3D"=
Gradient descent">Gradient descent</a> is a type of local search that optim=
izes a set of numerical parameters by incrementally adjusting them to minim=
ize a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loss_function" title=3D"Loss=
 function">loss function</a>. Variants of gradient descent are commonly use=
d to train <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_neural_netwo=
rk" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Artificial neural network">neural networ=
ks</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-82" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-82"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>76<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> through =
the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backpropagation" title=3D"Back=
propagation">backpropagation</a> algorithm.
</p><p>Another type of local search is <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Evolutionary_computation" title=3D"Evolutionary computation">evolution=
ary computation</a>, which aims to iteratively improve a set of candidate s=
olutions by "mutating" and "recombining" them, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Artificial_selection" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Artificia=
l selection">selecting</a> only the fittest to survive each generation.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-83" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-83"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[<=
/span>77<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Distributed search processes can coordinate via <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swarm_intelligence" title=3D"Swarm intelligence">swarm=
 intelligence</a> algorithms. Two popular swarm algorithms used in search a=
re <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Particle_swarm_optimization" ti=
tle=3D"Particle swarm optimization">particle swarm optimization</a> (inspir=
ed by bird <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flocking" title=3D"Floc=
king">flocking</a>) and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ant_colony=
_optimization" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Ant colony optimization">ant =
colony optimization</a> (inspired by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Ant_trail" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Ant trail">ant trails</a>).<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMerkleMiddendorf2013_84-0" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOO=
TNOTEMerkleMiddendorf2013-84"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>78<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Logic">Logic</h3></div>
<p>Formal <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logic" title=3D"Logic">l=
ogic</a> is used for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automatic_rea=
soning" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Automatic reasoning">reasoning</a> a=
nd <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knowledge_representation" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Knowledge representation">knowledge representatio=
n</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-85" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-85"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>79<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
Formal logic comes in two main forms: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Propositional_logic" title=3D"Propositional logic">propositional logic<=
/a> (which operates on statements that are true or false and uses <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logical_connective" title=3D"Logical conn=
ective">logical connectives</a> such as "and", "or", "not" and "implies")<s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-86" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-86"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>80<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Predicate_logic" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D=
"Predicate logic">predicate logic</a> (which also operates on objects, pred=
icates and relations and uses <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quan=
tifier_(logic)" title=3D"Quantifier (logic)">quantifiers</a> such as "<i>Ev=
ery</i> <i>X</i> is a <i>Y</i>" and "There are <i>some</i> <i>X</i>s that a=
re <i>Y</i>s").<sup id=3D"cite_ref-87" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-87"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>81<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deductive_reasoning" title=
=3D"Deductive reasoning">Deductive reasoning</a> in logic is the process of=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logical_proof" class=3D"mw-redire=
ct" title=3D"Logical proof">proving</a> a new statement (<a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logical_consequence" title=3D"Logical consequence">c=
onclusion</a>) from other statements that are given and assumed to be true =
(the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Premise" title=3D"Premise">pr=
emises</a>).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-88" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-88"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>82<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Pro=
ofs can be structured as proof <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tre=
e_structure" title=3D"Tree structure">trees</a>, in which nodes are labelle=
d by sentences, and children nodes are connected to parent nodes by <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inference_rule" class=3D"mw-redirect" tit=
le=3D"Inference rule">inference rules</a>.
</p><p>Given a problem and a set of premises, problem-solving reduces to se=
arching for a proof tree whose root node is labelled by a solution of the p=
roblem and whose <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leaf_nodes" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Leaf nodes">leaf nodes</a> are labelled by premis=
es or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom" title=3D"Axiom">axiom=
s</a>. In the case of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horn_clause"=
 title=3D"Horn clause">Horn clauses</a>, problem-solving search can be perf=
ormed by reasoning <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forward_chainin=
g" title=3D"Forward chaining">forwards</a> from the premises or <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backward_chaining" title=3D"Backward chaining=
">backwards</a> from the problem.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-89" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_not=
e-89"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>83<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]=
</span></a></sup> In the more general case of the clausal form of <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First-order_logic" title=3D"First-order l=
ogic">first-order logic</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resol=
ution_(logic)" title=3D"Resolution (logic)">resolution</a> is a single, axi=
om-free rule of inference, in which a problem is solved by proving a contra=
diction from premises that include the negation of the problem to be solved=
.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-90" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-90"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>84<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Inference in both Horn clause logic and first-order logic is <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Undecidable_problem" title=3D"Undecidable=
 problem">undecidable</a>, and therefore <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Intractable_problem" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Intractable prob=
lem">intractable</a>. However, backward reasoning with Horn clauses, which =
underpins computation in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logic=
_programming" title=3D"Logic programming">logic programming</a> language <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prolog" title=3D"Prolog">Prolog</a>,=
 is <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turing_complete" class=3D"mw-r=
edirect" title=3D"Turing complete">Turing complete</a>. Moreover, its effic=
iency is competitive with computation in other <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Symbolic_programming" title=3D"Symbolic programming">symbolic =
programming</a> languages.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-91" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-91"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>85<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuzzy_logic" title=3D"Fuzzy=
 logic">Fuzzy logic</a> assigns a "degree of truth" between 0 and 1. It can=
 therefore handle propositions that are vague and partially true.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-92" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-92"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
86<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-monotonic_logic" title=
=3D"Non-monotonic logic">Non-monotonic logics</a>, including logic programm=
ing with <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negation_as_failure" titl=
e=3D"Negation as failure">negation as failure</a>, are designed to handle <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Default_reasoning" class=3D"mw-redi=
rect" title=3D"Default reasoning">default reasoning</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref=
-Default_reasoning_30-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Default_reasoning-30"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>28<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> Other specialized versions of logic have been developed to describe man=
y complex domains.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Probabilistic_methods_for_u=
ncertain_reasoning">Probabilistic methods for uncertain reasoning</h3></div=
>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size skin-invert-image" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb=
