ing_of_Old_Faithful_data.gif" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"300" height=3D"25=
8" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"360" data-file-height=3D"30=
9"></a><figcaption><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expectation%E2%=
80%93maximization_algorithm" title=3D"Expectation=E2=80=93maximization algo=
rithm">Expectation=E2=80=93maximization</a> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Cluster_analysis" title=3D"Cluster analysis">clustering</a> of <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Faithful" title=3D"Old Faithful"=
>Old Faithful</a> eruption data starts from a random guess but then success=
fully converges on an accurate clustering of the two physically distinct mo=
des of eruption.</figcaption></figure>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Classifiers_and_statistical=
_learning_methods">Classifiers and statistical learning methods</h3></div>
<p>The simplest AI applications can be divided into two types: classifiers =
(e.g., "if shiny then diamond"), on one hand, and controllers (e.g., "if di=
amond then pick up"), on the other hand. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Classifier_(mathematics)" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Classifier =
(mathematics)">Classifiers</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref-107" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-=
107"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>99<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup> are functions that use <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Pattern_matching" title=3D"Pattern matching">pattern matching</a> to =
determine the closest match. They can be fine-tuned based on chosen example=
s using <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supervised_learning" title=
=3D"Supervised learning">supervised learning</a>. Each pattern (also called=
 an "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Random_variate" class=3D"mw-r=
edirect" title=3D"Random variate">observation</a>") is labeled with a certa=
in predefined class. All the observations combined with their class labels =
are known as a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_set" title=3D"=
Data set">data set</a>. When a new observation is received, that observatio=
n is classified based on previous experience.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Supervised=
_learning_51-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Supervised_learning-51"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>46<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>There are many kinds of classifiers in use.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-108" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_int=
elligence#cite_note-108"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>100<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Decision_tree" title=3D"Decision tree">decision tree</a> is the sim=
plest and most widely used symbolic machine learning algorithm.<sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-109" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
rtificial_intelligence#cite_note-109"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
101<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/K-nearest_neighbor" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"K-neare=
st neighbor">K-nearest neighbor</a> algorithm was the most widely used anal=
ogical AI until the mid-1990s, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Kernel_methods" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Kernel methods">Kernel meth=
ods</a> such as the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Support_vector=
_machine" title=3D"Support vector machine">support vector machine</a> (SVM)=
 displaced k-nearest neighbor in the 1990s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-110" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-110"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>102<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
The <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naive_Bayes_classifier" title=
=3D"Naive Bayes classifier">naive Bayes classifier</a> is reportedly the "m=
ost widely used learner"<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDomingos2015152_111-0" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_int=
elligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDomingos2015152-111"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>103<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> at Google, du=
e in part to its scalability.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-112" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-1=
12"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>104<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup>
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_neural_network" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Artificial neural network">Neural networks</a> ar=
e also used as classifiers.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Neural_networks_113-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-Neural_networks-113"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>10=
5<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Artificial_neural_networks"=
>Artificial neural networks</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size mw-halign-right" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Artificial_neural_network.svg=
" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wi=
kipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Artificial_neural_network.svg/250px-Artificial_n=
eural_network.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"223" clas=
s=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"560" data-file-height=3D"500"></a>=
<figcaption>A neural network is an interconnected group of nodes, akin to t=
he vast network of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuron" title=
=3D"Neuron">neurons</a> in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hum=
an_brain" title=3D"Human brain">human brain</a>.</figcaption></figure>
<p>An artificial neural network is based on a collection of nodes also know=
n as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_neurons" class=3D"=
mw-redirect" title=3D"Artificial neurons">artificial neurons</a>, which loo=
sely model the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neurons" class=3D"m=
w-redirect" title=3D"Neurons">neurons</a> in a biological brain. It is trai=
ned to recognise patterns; once trained, it can recognise those patterns in=
 fresh data. There is an input, at least one hidden layer of nodes and an o=
utput. Each node applies a function and once the <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Weighting" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Weighting">weight<=
/a> crosses its specified threshold, the data is transmitted to the next la=
yer. A network is typically called a deep neural network if it has at least=
 2 hidden layers.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Neural_networks_113-1" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_=
note-Neural_networks-113"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>105<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Learning algorithms for neural networks use <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Local_search_(optimization)" title=3D"Local search (optimi=
zation)">local search</a> to choose the weights that will get the right out=
put for each input during training. The most common training technique is t=
he <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backpropagation" title=3D"Backp=
ropagation">backpropagation</a> algorithm.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-114" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligenc=
e#cite_note-114"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>106<span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Neural networks learn to model complex relati=
onships between inputs and outputs and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Pattern_recognition" title=3D"Pattern recognition">find patterns</a> i=
n data. In theory, a neural network can learn any function.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-115" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artif=
icial_intelligence#cite_note-115"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>107<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feedforward_neural_netwo=
rk" title=3D"Feedforward neural network">feedforward neural networks</a> th=
e signal passes in only one direction.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-116" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#ci=
te_note-116"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>108<span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">]</span></a></sup> The term <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Perceptron" title=3D"Perceptron">perceptron</a> typically refers to a sing=
le-layer neural network.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-117" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-117"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>109<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span=
></a></sup> In contrast, deep learning uses many layers.<sup id=3D"cite_ref=
-Deep_learning_118-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Deep_learning-118"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>110<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recurrent_neural_network" title=3D"=
Recurrent neural network">Recurrent neural networks</a> (RNNs) feed the out=
put signal back into the input, which allows short-term memories of previou=
s input events. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_short-term_me=
mory" title=3D"Long short-term memory">Long short-term memory</a> networks =
(LSTMs) are recurrent neural networks that better preserve longterm depende=
ncies and are less sensitive to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Vanishing_gradient_problem" title=3D"Vanishing gradient problem">vanishin=
g gradient problem</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-119" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-119"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>111<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convolutional_neural_ne=
twork" title=3D"Convolutional neural network">Convolutional neural networks=
</a> (CNNs) use layers of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kernel_(=
image_processing)" title=3D"Kernel (image processing)">kernels</a> to more =
efficiently process local patterns. This local processing is especially imp=
ortant in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image_processing" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Image processing">image processing</a>, where the=
 early CNN layers typically identify simple local patterns such as edges an=
d curves, with subsequent layers detecting more complex patterns like textu=
res, and eventually whole objects.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-120" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_n=
ote-120"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>112<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Deep_learning">Deep learnin=
g</h3></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:AI-ML-DL.svg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><=
img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/AI-ML-=
DL.svg/250px-AI-ML-DL.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"190" height=3D"2=
10" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"748" data-file-height=3D"8=
26"></a><figcaption><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_learning"=
 title=3D"Deep learning">Deep learning</a> is a subset of <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning" title=3D"Machine learning">machin=
e learning</a>, which is itself a subset of artificial intelligence.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-journalimcms.org_121-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-journalimcms.org-1=
21"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>113<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_learning" title=3D"Deep le=
arning">Deep learning</a> uses several layers of neurons between the networ=
k's inputs and outputs.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Deep_learning_118-1" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#c=
ite_note-Deep_learning-118"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>110<span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The multiple layers can progressiv=
ely extract higher-level features from the raw input. For example, in <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Image_processing" class=3D"mw-redirect"=
 title=3D"Image processing">image processing</a>, lower layers may identify=
 edges, while higher layers may identify the concepts relevant to a human s=
uch as digits, letters, or faces.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDengYu2014199=
=E2=80=93200_122-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDengYu2014199%E2%80%93200-1=
22"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>114<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup>
</p><p>Deep learning has profoundly improved the performance of programs in=
 many important subfields of artificial intelligence, including <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_vision" title=3D"Computer vision">co=
mputer vision</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speech_recognit=
ion" title=3D"Speech recognition">speech recognition</a>, <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Natural_language_processing" title=3D"Natural langu=
age processing">natural language processing</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Image_classification" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Image c=
lassification">image classification</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECiresan=
MeierSchmidhuber2012_123-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTECiresanMeierSchmidh=
uber2012-123"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>115<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup> and others. The reason that deep learning perfor=
ms so well in so many applications is not known as of 2021.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021750_124-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussell=
Norvig2021750-124"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>116<span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The sudden success of deep learning in 2012=
=E2=80=932015 did not occur because of some new discovery or theoretical br=
eakthrough (deep neural networks and backpropagation had been described by =
many people, as far back as the 1950s)<sup id=3D"cite_ref-131" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#ci=
te_note-131"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>i<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup> but because of two factors: the incredible increase=
 in computer power (including the hundred-fold increase in speed by switchi=
ng to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPU" class=3D"mw-redirect" t=
itle=3D"GPU">GPUs</a>) and the availability of vast amounts of training dat=
a, especially the giant <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_da=
tasets_for_machine-learning_research" title=3D"List of datasets for machine=
-learning research">curated datasets</a> used for benchmark testing, such a=
s <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ImageNet" title=3D"ImageNet">Ima=
geNet</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-133" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-133"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>j<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"GPT">GPT</h3></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generative_pre-trained_transfor=
mer" title=3D"Generative pre-trained transformer">Generative pre-trained tr=
ansformers</a> (GPT) are <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_lan=
guage_model" title=3D"Large language model">large language models</a> (LLMs=
) that generate text based on the semantic relationships between words in s=
entences. Text-based GPT models are pre-trained on a large <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corpus_of_text" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Cor=
pus of text">corpus of text</a> that can be from the Internet. The pretrain=
ing consists of predicting the next <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Lexical_analysis" title=3D"Lexical analysis">token</a> (a token being usu=
ally a word, subword, or punctuation). Throughout this pretraining, GPT mod=
els accumulate knowledge about the world and can then generate human-like t=
ext by repeatedly predicting the next token. Typically, a subsequent traini=
ng phase makes the model more truthful, useful, and harmless, usually with =
a technique called <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reinforcement_l=
earning_from_human_feedback" title=3D"Reinforcement learning from human fee=
dback">reinforcement learning from human feedback</a> (RLHF). Current GPT m=
odels are prone to generating falsehoods called "<a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Hallucination_(artificial_intelligence)" title=3D"Hallucinat=
ion (artificial intelligence)">hallucinations</a>". These can be reduced wi=
th RLHF and quality data, but the problem has been getting worse for reason=
ing systems.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-134" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-134"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>124<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> Such systems are used in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chatbot=
" title=3D"Chatbot">chatbots</a>, which allow people to ask a question or r=
equest a task in simple text.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESmith2023_135-0" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTESmith2023-135"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>125<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-1=
36" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial=
_intelligence#cite_note-136"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>126<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Current models and services include <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/ChatGPT" title=3D"ChatGPT">ChatGPT</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Anthropic#Claude" title=3D"Anthropic">Claude</a>, <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemini_(chatbot)" class=3D"mw-redirect" titl=
e=3D"Gemini (chatbot)">Gemini</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Microsoft_Copilot" title=3D"Microsoft Copilot">Copilot</a>, and <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta_AI" title=3D"Meta AI">Meta AI</a>.<sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-137" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-137"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>127<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multimodal_learning" title=3D"Multimodal learning">=
Multimodal</a> GPT models can process different types of data (<a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modality_(human%E2%80%93computer_interaction)"=
 title=3D"Modality (human=E2=80=93computer interaction)">modalities</a>) su=
ch as images, videos, sound, and text.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMarmouyet=
2023_138-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMarmouyet2023-138"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>128<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Hardware_and_software">Hard=
ware and software</h3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programming_languag=
es_for_artificial_intelligence" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Programming =
languages for artificial intelligence">Programming languages for artificial=
 intelligence</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardware_for=
_artificial_intelligence" title=3D"Hardware for artificial intelligence">Ha=
rdware for artificial intelligence</a></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Raspberry_Pi_AI_Kit_for_Raspberry_Pi_5_comple=
te_Kit_07.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wik=
imedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Raspberry_Pi_AI_Kit_for_Raspberry_P=
i_5_complete_Kit_07.jpg/250px-Raspberry_Pi_AI_Kit_for_Raspberry_Pi_5_comple=
te_Kit_07.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"167" class=3D"mw-=
file-element" data-file-width=3D"4831" data-file-height=3D"3221"></a><figca=
ption>Raspberry Pi AI Kit</figcaption></figure>
<p>In the late 2010s, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graphics_pro=
cessing_unit" title=3D"Graphics processing unit">graphics processing units<=
/a> (GPUs) that were increasingly designed with AI-specific enhancements an=
d used with specialized <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TensorFlow=
" title=3D"TensorFlow">TensorFlow</a> software had replaced previously used=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_processing_unit" title=3D=
"Central processing unit">central processing unit</a> (CPUs) as the dominan=
t means for large-scale (commercial and academic) <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning" title=3D"Machine learning">machine learni=
ng</a> models' training.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKobielus2019_139-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKobielus2019-139"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>129<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Specialized <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programming_language" title=3D"Programmin=
