<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Agents">Agents</h3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agentic_AI" class=3D=
"mw-redirect" title=3D"Agentic AI">Agentic AI</a></div>
<p>AI agents are software entities designed to perceive their environment, =
make decisions, and take actions autonomously to achieve specific goals. Th=
ese agents can interact with users, their environment, or other agents. AI =
agents are used in various applications, including <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_assistant" title=3D"Virtual assistant">virtual ass=
istants</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chatbots" class=3D"mw=
-redirect" title=3D"Chatbots">chatbots</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Autonomous_vehicles" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Autonomous ve=
hicles">autonomous vehicles</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V=
ideo_game_console" title=3D"Video game console">game-playing systems</a>, a=
nd <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Industrial_robotics" class=3D"m=
w-redirect" title=3D"Industrial robotics">industrial robotics</a>. AI agent=
s operate within the constraints of their programming, available computatio=
nal resources, and hardware limitations. This means they are restricted to =
performing tasks within their defined scope and have finite memory and proc=
essing capabilities. In real-world applications, AI agents often face time =
constraints for decision-making and action execution. Many AI agents incorp=
orate learning algorithms, enabling them to improve their performance over =
time through experience or training. Using machine learning, AI agents can =
adapt to new situations and optimise their behaviour for their designated t=
asks.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-183" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-183"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>173<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-184" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-184"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>174<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-=
185" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificia=
l_intelligence#cite_note-185"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>175<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Web_search">Web search</h3>=
</div>
<p>Microsoft introduced <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft_=
Copilot" title=3D"Microsoft Copilot">Copilot Search</a> in February 2023 un=
der the name <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bing_Chat" class=3D"m=
w-redirect" title=3D"Bing Chat">Bing Chat</a>. Copilot Search provides AI-g=
enerated summaries.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-186" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-186"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>176<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup>
</p><p>Google introduced an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_Mod=
e" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"AI Mode">AI Mode</a> at its Google I/O ev=
ent on 20 May 2025.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-187" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-187"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>177<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Sexuality">Sexuality</h3></=
div>
<p>Applications of AI in this domain include AI-enabled menstruation and fe=
rtility trackers that analyze user data to offer predictions,<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-188" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art=
ificial_intelligence#cite_note-188"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>17=
8<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> AI-integrated sex toys (e.=
g., <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teledildonics" title=3D"Teledi=
ldonics">teledildonics</a>),<sup id=3D"cite_ref-189" class=3D"reference"><a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-18=
9"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>179<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup> AI-generated sexual education content,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-1=
90" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial=
_intelligence#cite_note-190"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>180<span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and AI agents that simulate sexua=
l and romantic partners (e.g., <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rep=
lika" title=3D"Replika">Replika</a>).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-191" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cit=
e_note-191"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>181<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup> AI is also used for the production of non-consensu=
al <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deepfake_pornography" title=3D"=
Deepfake pornography">deepfake pornography</a>, raising significant ethical=
 and legal concerns.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-192" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-192"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>182<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a=
></sup>
</p><p>AI technologies have also been used to attempt to identify <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Online_gender-based_violence" title=3D"On=
line gender-based violence">online gender-based violence</a> and online <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sexual_grooming" title=3D"Sexual groo=
ming">sexual grooming</a> of minors.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-193" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite=
_note-193"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>183<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-194" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-194"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>184<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Other_industry-specific_tas=
ks">Other industry-specific tasks</h3></div>
<p>In a 2017 survey, one in five companies reported having incorporated "AI=
" in some offerings or processes.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-195" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_no=
te-195"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>185<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup>=20
</p><p>In the field of evacuation and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Disaster" title=3D"Disaster">disaster</a> managemen, AI has been used t=
o investigate patterns in large-scale and small-scale evacuations using his=
torical data from GPS, videos or social media.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-196" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-196"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>186<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-197" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_no=
te-197"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>187<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-198" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-198"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>188<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a=
></sup>
</p><p>During the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2024_Indian_gene=
ral_election" title=3D"2024 Indian general election">2024 Indian elections<=
/a>, US$50 million was spent on authorized AI-generated content, notably by=
 creating <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deepfake" title=3D"Deepf=
ake">deepfakes</a> of allied (including sometimes deceased) politicians to =
better engage with voters, and by translating speeches to various local lan=
guages.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-199" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-199"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>189<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Ethics">Ethics</h2></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethics_of_artificial=
_intelligence" title=3D"Ethics of artificial intelligence">Ethics of artifi=
cial intelligence</a></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:ChatGPT_street_art_in_Tel_Aviv.jpg" class=3D"=
mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/com=
mons/thumb/8/8e/ChatGPT_street_art_in_Tel_Aviv.jpg/250px-ChatGPT_street_art=
_in_Tel_Aviv.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"321" class=3D"=
mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"1755" data-file-height=3D"2256"></a><fi=
gcaption><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Street_art_in_Tel_Aviv" c=
lass=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Street art in Tel Aviv">Street art in Tel Avi=
v</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref-200" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-200"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>190<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-201" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-201"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>191<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup></figcaption></figur=
e>
<p>AI has potential benefits and potential risks.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-202" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-202"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>192<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> AI may be able to advance science and =
find solutions for serious problems: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Demis_Hassabis" title=3D"Demis Hassabis">Demis Hassabis</a> of <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeepMind" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"=
DeepMind">DeepMind</a> hopes to "solve intelligence, and then use that to s=
olve everything else".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESimonite2016_203-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-FOOTNOTESimonite2016-203"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>193<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> However, as the use o=
f AI has become widespread, several unintended consequences and risks have =
been identified.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021987_204-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021987-204"><span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">[</span>194<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-205" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
rtificial_intelligence#cite_note-205"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
195<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In-production systems ca=
n sometimes not factor ethics and bias into their AI training processes, es=
pecially when the AI algorithms are inherently unexplainable in deep learni=
ng.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTELaskowski2023_206-0" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOO=
TNOTELaskowski2023-206"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>196<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Risks_and_harm">Risks and h=
arm</h3></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Privacy_and_copyright">Priv=
acy and copyright</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Further information: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_p=
rivacy" title=3D"Information privacy">Information privacy</a> and <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence_and_copyright" ti=
tle=3D"Artificial intelligence and copyright">Artificial intelligence and c=
opyright</a></div>
<p>Machine learning algorithms require large amounts of data. The technique=
s used to acquire this data have raised concerns about <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Privacy" title=3D"Privacy">privacy</a>, <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surveillance" title=3D"Surveillance">surveillanc=
e</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copyright" title=3D"Copy=
right">copyright</a>.
</p><p>AI-powered devices and services, such as virtual assistants and IoT =
products, continuously collect personal information, raising concerns about=
 intrusive data gathering and unauthorized access by third parties. The los=
s of privacy is further exacerbated by AI's ability to process and combine =
vast amounts of data, potentially leading to a surveillance society where i=
ndividual activities are constantly monitored and analyzed without adequate=
 safeguards or transparency.
</p><p>Sensitive user data collected may include online activity records, g=
eolocation data, video, or audio.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGAO2022_207-0"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_in=
telligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGAO2022-207"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>197<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> For example, in orde=
r to build <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speech_recognition" tit=
le=3D"Speech recognition">speech recognition</a> algorithms, <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_(company)" title=3D"Amazon (company)">Ama=
zon</a> has recorded millions of private conversations and allowed <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temporary_worker" class=3D"mw-redirect" t=
itle=3D"Temporary worker">temporary workers</a> to listen to and transcribe=
 some of them.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEValinsky2019_208-0" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cit=
e_note-FOOTNOTEValinsky2019-208"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>198<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Opinions about this widesprea=
d surveillance range from those who see it as a <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Necessary_evil" title=3D"Necessary evil">necessary evil</a> t=
o those for whom it is clearly <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Une=
thical" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Unethical">unethical</a> and a viola=
tion of the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_to_privacy" titl=
e=3D"Right to privacy">right to privacy</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERus=
sellNorvig2021991_209-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021991-2=
09"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>199<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup>
</p><p>AI developers argue that this is the only way to deliver valuable ap=
plications and have developed several techniques that attempt to preserve p=
rivacy while still obtaining the data, such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Data_aggregation" title=3D"Data aggregation">data aggregation<=
/a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De-identification" title=3D"D=
e-identification">de-identification</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Differential_privacy" title=3D"Differential privacy">differential=
 privacy</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021991=E2=80=93992_2=
10-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifici=
al_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021991%E2%80%93992-210"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>200<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span><=
/a></sup> Since 2016, some privacy experts, such as <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Cynthia_Dwork" title=3D"Cynthia Dwork">Cynthia Dwork</a>,=
 have begun to view privacy in terms of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Fairness_(machine_learning)" title=3D"Fairness (machine learning)">fa=
irness</a>. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brian_Christian" title=
=3D"Brian Christian">Brian Christian</a> wrote that experts have pivoted "f=
rom the question of 'what they know' to the question of 'what they're doing=
 with it'."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChristian202063_211-0" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cit=
e_note-FOOTNOTEChristian202063-211"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>20=
1<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Generative AI is often trained on unlicensed copyrighted works, incl=
uding in domains such as images or computer code; the output is then used u=
nder the rationale of "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fair_use" t=
itle=3D"Fair use">fair use</a>". Experts disagree about how well and under =
what circumstances this rationale will hold up in courts of law; relevant f=
actors may include "the purpose and character of the use of the copyrighted=
 work" and "the effect upon the potential market for the copyrighted work".=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEVincent2022_212-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOT=
EVincent2022-212"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>202<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-213" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-=
213"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>203<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]=
</span></a></sup> Website owners can indicate that they do not want their c=
ontent scraped via a "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robots.txt" =
title=3D"Robots.txt">robots.txt</a>" file.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-214" class=3D=
"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligenc=
e#cite_note-214"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>204<span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">]</span></a></sup> However, some companies will scrape content r=
egardless<sup id=3D"cite_ref-215" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-215"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>205<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-216" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-216"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>206<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> because the robo=
ts.txt file has no real authority. In 2023, leading authors (including <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Grisham" title=3D"John Grisham">J=
ohn Grisham</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jonathan_Franz=
en" title=3D"Jonathan Franzen">Jonathan Franzen</a>) sued AI companies for =
using their work to train generative AI.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEReisner=
2023_217-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEReisner2023-217"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>207<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAlterHarris2023_218-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAl=
terHarris2023-218"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>208<span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Another discussed approach is to envision a=
 separate <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sui_generis" title=3D=
"Sui generis">sui generis</a></i> system of protection for creations genera=
ted by AI to ensure fair attribution and compensation for human authors.<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-219" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-219"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>209<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Dominance_by_tech_giants">D=
ominance by tech giants</h4></div>
<p>The commercial AI scene is dominated by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Big_Tech" title=3D"Big Tech">Big Tech</a> companies such as <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alphabet_Inc." title=3D"Alphabet Inc.">A=
lphabet Inc.</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_(company)=
" title=3D"Amazon (company)">Amazon</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Apple_Inc." title=3D"Apple Inc.">Apple Inc.</a>, <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta_Platforms" title=3D"Meta Platforms">Meta Platfor=
ms</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft" title=3D"Mi=
crosoft">Microsoft</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-220" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-220"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>210<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-221" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-221"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>211<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-222" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-222"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>212<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Some of these p=
layers already own the vast majority of existing <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Cloud_computing" title=3D"Cloud computing">cloud infrastruct=
ure</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computing" title=3D"Co=
mputing">computing</a> power from <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Data_center" title=3D"Data center">data centers</a>, allowing them to entre=
nch further in the marketplace.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-223" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note=
-223"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>213<span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-224" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-224"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>214<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Power_needs_and_environment=
al_impacts">Power needs and environmental impacts</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
See also: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Environmental_impacts_of=
_artificial_intelligence" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Environmental impa=
cts of artificial intelligence">Environmental impacts of artificial intelli=
gence</a></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:2015-_Data_center_power_demand_-_US.svg" clas=
s=3D"mw-file-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedi=
a/commons/thumb/1/16/2015-_Data_center_power_demand_-_US.svg/250px-2015-_Da=
ta_center_power_demand_-_US.svg.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" heigh=
t=3D"141" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"1200" data-file-heig=
ht=3D"675"></a><figcaption>Fueled by growth in artificial intelligence, dat=
a centers' demand for power increased in the 2020s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Wash=
Post_20251225_225-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-WashPost_20251225-225"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>215<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
></figcaption></figure>
<p>In January 2024, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internatio=
nal_Energy_Agency" title=3D"International Energy Agency">International Ener=
gy Agency</a> (IEA) released <i>Electricity 2024, Analysis and Forecast to =
2026</i>, forecasting electric power use.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-226" class=3D"=
reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence=
#cite_note-226"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>216<span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">]</span></a></sup> This is the first IEA report to make projectio=
ns for data centers and power consumption for artificial intelligence and c=
ryptocurrency. The report states that power demand for these uses might dou=
ble by 2026, with additional electric power usage equal to electricity used=
 by the whole Japanese nation.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-227" class=3D"reference">=
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-=
227"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>217<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]=
</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Prodigious power consumption by AI is responsible for the growth of =
fossil fuel use, and might delay closings of obsolete, carbon-emitting coal=
 energy facilities. There is a feverish rise in the construction of data ce=
nters throughout the US, making large technology firms (e.g., Microsoft, Me=
ta, Google, Amazon) into voracious consumers of electric power. Projected e=
lectric consumption is so immense that there is concern that it will be ful=
filled no matter the source. A ChatGPT search involves the use of 10 times =
the electrical energy as a Google search. The large firms are in haste to f=
ind power sources =E2=80=93 from nuclear energy to geothermal to fusion. Th=
e tech firms argue that =E2=80=93 in the long view =E2=80=93 AI will be eve=
ntually kinder to the environment, but they need the energy now. AI makes t=
he power grid more efficient and "intelligent", will assist in the growth o=
f nuclear power, and track overall carbon emissions, according to technolog=
y firms.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-228" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-228"><span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">[</span>218<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>A 2024 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goldman_Sachs" title=
=3D"Goldman Sachs">Goldman Sachs</a> Research Paper, <i>AI Data Centers and=
 the Coming US Power Demand Surge</i>, found "US power demand (is) likely t=
o experience growth not seen in a generation...." and forecasts that, by 20=
30, US data centers will consume 8% of US power, as opposed to 3% in 2022, =
presaging growth for the electrical power generation industry by a variety =
of means.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-229" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-229"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>219<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Dat=
a centers' need for more and more electrical power is such that they might =
max out the electrical grid. The Big Tech companies counter that AI can be =
used to maximize the utilization of the grid by all.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-230=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_i=
ntelligence#cite_note-230"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>220<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In 2024, the <i>Wall Street Journal</i> reported that big AI compani=
es have begun negotiations with the US nuclear power providers to provide e=
lectricity to the data centers. In March 2024 Amazon purchased a Pennsylvan=
ia nuclear-powered data center for US$650 million.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-231" =
class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_int=
elligence#cite_note-231"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>221<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Nvidia" title=3D"Nvidia">Nvidia</a> CEO <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Jensen_Huang" title=3D"Jensen Huang">Jensen Huang</a> said nuclea=
r power is a good option for the data centers.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-232" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-232"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>222<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In September 2024, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsof=
t" title=3D"Microsoft">Microsoft</a> announced an agreement with <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constellation_Energy" title=3D"Constellation=
 Energy">Constellation Energy</a> to re-open the <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Three_Mile_Island" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Three Mile=
 Island">Three Mile Island</a> nuclear power plant to provide Microsoft wit=
h 100% of all electric power produced by the plant for 20 years. Reopening =
the plant, which suffered a partial nuclear meltdown of its Unit 2 reactor =
in 1979, will require Constellation to get through strict regulatory proces=
ses which will include extensive safety scrutiny from the US <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_Regulatory_Commission" title=3D"Nuclear =
Regulatory Commission">Nuclear Regulatory Commission</a>. If approved (this=
 will be the first ever US re-commissioning of a nuclear plant), over 835 m=
egawatts of power =E2=80=93 enough for 800,000 homes =E2=80=93 of energy wi=
ll be produced. The cost for re-opening and upgrading is estimated at US$1.=
6 billion and is dependent on tax breaks for nuclear power contained in the=
 2022 US <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inflation_Reduction_Act" =
title=3D"Inflation Reduction Act">Inflation Reduction Act</a>.<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-233" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-233"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>2=
23<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The US government and the=
 state of Michigan are investing almost US$2 billion to reopen the <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palisades_Nuclear_Generating_Station" tit=
le=3D"Palisades Nuclear Generating Station">Palisades Nuclear</a> reactor o=
n Lake Michigan. Closed since 2022, the plant is planned to be reopened in =
October 2025. The Three Mile Island facility will be renamed the Crane Clea=
n Energy Center after Chris Crane, a nuclear proponent and former CEO of <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exelon" title=3D"Exelon">Exelon</a> =
who was responsible for Exelon's spinoff of Constellation.<sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-234" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence#cite_note-234"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>224<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>After the last approval in September 2023, <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Taiwan" title=3D"Taiwan">Taiwan</a> suspended the approval =
of data centers north of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taoyuan,_=
Taiwan" title=3D"Taoyuan, Taiwan">Taoyuan</a> with a capacity of more than =
5 MW in 2024, due to power supply shortages.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-DatacenterD=
ynamics_235-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-DatacenterDynamics-235"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>225<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Tai=
wan aims to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_power_phase-ou=
t" title=3D"Nuclear power phase-out">phase out nuclear power</a> by 2025.<s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-DatacenterDynamics_235-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-DatacenterD=
ynamics-235"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>225<span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">]</span></a></sup> On the other hand, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Singapore" title=3D"Singapore">Singapore</a> imposed a ban on th=
e opening of data centers in 2019 due to electric power, but in 2022, lifte=
d this ban.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-DatacenterDynamics_235-2" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_not=
e-DatacenterDynamics-235"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>225<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Although most nuclear plants in Japan have been shut down after the =
2011 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fukushima_nuclear_accident" t=
itle=3D"Fukushima nuclear accident">Fukushima nuclear accident</a>, accordi=
ng to an October 2024 <i>Bloomberg</i> article in Japanese, cloud gaming se=
rvices company Ubitus, in which Nvidia has a stake, is looking for land in =
Japan near a nuclear power plant for a new data center for generative AI.<s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-bloombergjp_236-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-bloombergjp-236"><=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>226<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span=
></a></sup> Ubitus CEO Wesley Kuo said nuclear power plants are the most ef=
ficient, cheap and stable power for AI.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-bloombergjp_236-=
1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_=
intelligence#cite_note-bloombergjp-236"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>226<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>On 1 November 2024, the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fed=
eral_Energy_Regulatory_Commission" title=3D"Federal Energy Regulatory Commi=
ssion">Federal Energy Regulatory Commission</a> (FERC) rejected an applicat=
ion submitted by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talen_Energy" tit=
le=3D"Talen Energy">Talen Energy</a> for approval to supply some electricit=
y from the nuclear power station <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S=
usquehanna_Steam_Electric_Station" title=3D"Susquehanna Steam Electric Stat=
ion">Susquehanna</a> to Amazon's data center.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Bloomberg2=
0241104_237-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Bloomberg20241104-237"><span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">[</span>227<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
According to the Commission Chairman <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Willie_L._Phillips" title=3D"Willie L. Phillips">Willie L. Phillips</a>,=
 it is a burden on the electricity grid as well as a significant cost shift=
ing concern to households and other business sectors.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Bl=
oomberg20241104_237-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Bloomberg20241104-237"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>227<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up>
</p><p>In 2025, a report prepared by the International Energy Agency estima=
ted the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenhouse_gas_emissions" =
title=3D"Greenhouse gas emissions">greenhouse gas emissions</a> from the en=
ergy consumption of AI at 180 million tons. By 2035, these emissions could =
rise to 300=E2=80=93500 million tonnes depending on what measures will be t=
aken. This is below 1.5% of the energy sector emissions. The emissions redu=
ction potential of AI was estimated at 5% of the energy sector emissions, b=
ut <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rebound_effect_(conservation)" =
title=3D"Rebound effect (conservation)">rebound effects</a> (for example if=
 people switch from public transport to autonomous cars) can reduce it.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-238" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-238"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>228<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Misinformation">Misinformat=
ion</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
See also: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content_moderation" titl=
e=3D"Content moderation">Content moderation</a></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/YouTube" title=3D"YouTube">YouT=
ube</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook" title=3D"Facebo=
ok">Facebook</a> and others use <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Re=
commender_system" title=3D"Recommender system">recommender systems</a> to g=
uide users to more content. These AI programs were given the goal of <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mathematical_optimization" title=3D"Math=
ematical optimization">maximizing</a> user engagement (that is, the only go=
al was to keep people watching). The AI learned that users tended to choose=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Misinformation" title=3D"Misinfor=
mation">misinformation</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conspi=
racy_theories" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Conspiracy theories">conspira=
cy theories</a>, and extreme <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parti=
san_(politics)" title=3D"Partisan (politics)">partisan</a> content, and, to=
 keep them watching, the AI recommended more of it. Users also tended to wa=
tch more content on the same subject, so the AI led people into <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filter_bubbles" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"Filter bubbles">filter bubbles</a> where they received multiple version=
s of the same misinformation.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTENicas2018_239-0" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTENicas2018-239"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>229<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> This convinced many =
users that the misinformation was true, and ultimately undermined trust in =
institutions, the media and the government.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-240" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-240"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>230<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The AI program had correctly learned to ma=
ximize its goal, but the result was harmful to society. After the U.S. elec=
tion in 2016, major technology companies took some steps to mitigate the pr=
oblem.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-241" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-241"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>231<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In the early 2020s, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generat=
ive_AI" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Generative AI">generative AI</a> beg=
an to create images, audio, and texts that are virtually indistinguishable =
from real photographs, recordings, or human writing,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-242=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_i=
ntelligence#cite_note-242"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>232<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> while realistic AI-generated videos=
 became feasible in the mid-2020s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-243" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_n=
ote-243"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>233<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-244" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-244"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>234<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-245" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-245"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>235<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> It=
 is possible for bad actors to use this technology to create massive amount=
s of misinformation or propaganda;<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWilliams2023_=
246-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWilliams2023-246"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>236<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> one such =
potential malicious use is deepfakes for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Computational_propaganda" title=3D"Computational propaganda">computa=
tional propaganda</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-247" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-247"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>237<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> AI pioneer and Nobel Prize-winning computer scientist <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoffrey_Hinton" title=3D"Geoffrey Hinton">G=
eoffrey Hinton</a> expressed concern about AI enabling "authoritarian leade=
rs to manipulate their electorates" on a large scale, among other risks.<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTETaylorHern2023_248-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOT=
ETaylorHern2023-248"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>238<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The ability to influence electorates has =
been proved in at least one study. This same study shows more inaccurate st=
atements from the models when they advocate for candidates of the political=
 right.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-249" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-249"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>239<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>AI researchers at <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsoft=
" title=3D"Microsoft">Microsoft</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/OpenAI" title=3D"OpenAI">OpenAI</a>, universities and other organisation=
s have suggested using "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proof_of_p=
ersonhood#Approaches" title=3D"Proof of personhood">personhood credentials<=
/a>" as a way to overcome online deception enabled by AI models.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-250" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-250"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>240<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Algorithmic_bias_and_fairne=
ss">Algorithmic bias and fairness</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithmic_bias" t=
itle=3D"Algorithmic bias">Algorithmic bias</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Fairness_(machine_learning)" title=3D"Fairness (machine le=
arning)">Fairness (machine learning)</a></div>
<p>Machine learning applications can be <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Algorithmic_bias" title=3D"Algorithmic bias">biased</a><sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-252" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art=
ificial_intelligence#cite_note-252"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>k<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> if they learn from biased da=
ta.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERose2023_253-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOT=
ERose2023-253"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>242<span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">]</span></a></sup> The developers may not be aware that the bias e=
xists.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTECNA2019_254-0" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNO=
TECNA2019-254"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>243<span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">]</span></a></sup> Discriminatory behavior by some LLMs can be obs=
erved in their output.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-255" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-255"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>244<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> Bias can be introduced by the way <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Training_data" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Training data">tra=
ining data</a> is selected and by the way a model is deployed.<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-FOOTNOTEGoffrey200817_256-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoffrey2008=
17-256"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>245<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERose2023_253-1" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#c=
ite_note-FOOTNOTERose2023-253"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>242<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> If a biased algorithm is used t=
o make decisions that can seriously <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Harm" title=3D"Harm">harm</a> people (as it can in <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Health_equity" title=3D"Health equity">medicine</a>, <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Credit_rating" title=3D"Credit rating=
">finance</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recruitment" title=
=3D"Recruitment">recruitment</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Public_housing" title=3D"Public housing">housing</a> or <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Policing" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Policing">po=
licing</a>) then the algorithm may cause <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Discrimination" title=3D"Discrimination">discrimination</a>.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-257" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-257"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>246<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The field of <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairness_(machine_learning)" title=3D"Fa=
irness (machine learning)">fairness</a> studies how to prevent harms from a=
lgorithmic biases.
</p><p>On 28 June 2015, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_Pho=
tos" title=3D"Google Photos">Google Photos</a>'s new image labeling feature=
 mistakenly identified Jacky Alcine and a friend as "gorillas" because they=
 were black. The system was trained on a dataset that contained very few im=
ages of black people,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChristian202025_258-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChristian202025-258"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>247<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> a problem called=
 "sample size disparity".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021995_2=
59-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifici=
al_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021995-259"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>248<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Go=
ogle "fixed" this problem by preventing the system from labelling <i>anythi=
ng</i> as a "gorilla". Eight years later, in 2023, Google Photos still coul=
d not identify a gorilla, and neither could similar products from Apple, Fa=
cebook, Microsoft and Amazon.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGrantHill2023_260-=
0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_=
intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGrantHill2023-260"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>249<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COMPAS_(software)" title=3D=
"COMPAS (software)">COMPAS</a> is a commercial program widely used by <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._court" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"U.S. court">U.S. courts</a> to assess the likelihood of a <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Defendant" title=3D"Defendant">defendant</a> be=
coming a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recidivist" class=3D"mw-r=
edirect" title=3D"Recidivist">recidivist</a>. In 2016, <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julia_Angwin" title=3D"Julia Angwin">Julia Angwin</a> =
at <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ProPublica" title=3D"ProPublica=
">ProPublica</a> discovered that COMPAS exhibited racial bias, despite the =
fact that the program was not told the races of the defendants. Although th=
e error rate for both whites and blacks was calibrated equal at exactly 61%=
, the errors for each race were different=E2=80=94the system consistently o=
verestimated the chance that a black person would re-offend and would under=
estimate the chance that a white person would not re-offend.<sup id=3D"cite=
_ref-FOOTNOTELarsonAngwin2016_261-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTELarsonAngw=
in2016-261"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>250<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup> In 2017, several researchers<sup id=3D"cite_ref-26=
3" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_=
intelligence#cite_note-263"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>l<span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> showed that it was mathematically im=
possible for COMPAS to accommodate all possible measures of fairness when t=
he base rates of re-offense were different for whites and blacks in the dat=
a.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-264" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-264"><span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">[</span>252<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>A program can make biased decisions even if the data does not explic=
itly mention a problematic feature (such as "race" or "gender"). The featur=
e will correlate with other features (like "address", "shopping history" or=
 "first name"), and the program will make the same decisions based on these=
 features as it would on "race" or "gender".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-265" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-265"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>253<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Moritz Hardt said "the most robust fact in=
 this research area is that fairness through blindness doesn't work."<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-266" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-266"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[<=
/span>254<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Criticism of COMPAS highlighted that machine learning models are des=
igned to make "predictions" that are only valid if we assume that the futur=
e will resemble the past. If they are trained on data that includes the res=
ults of racist decisions in the past, machine learning models must predict =
that racist decisions will be made in the future. If an application then us=
es these predictions as <i>recommendations</i>, some of these "recommendati=
ons" will likely be racist.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-267" class=3D"reference"><a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-267=
"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>255<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</s=
pan></a></sup> Thus, machine learning is not well suited to help make decis=
ions in areas where there is hope that the future will be <i>better</i> tha=
n the past. It is descriptive rather than prescriptive.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-=
269" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificia=
l_intelligence#cite_note-269"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>m<span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Bias and unfairness may go undetected because the developers are ove=
rwhelmingly white and male: among AI engineers, about 4% are black and 20% =
are women.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021995_259-1" class=3D"=
reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence=
#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021995-259"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>248<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>There are various conflicting definitions and mathematical models of=
 fairness. These notions depend on ethical assumptions, and are influenced =
by beliefs about society. One broad category is <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Distributive_justice" title=3D"Distributive justice">distribu=
tive fairness</a>, which focuses on the outcomes, often identifying groups =
and seeking to compensate for statistical disparities. Representational fai=
rness tries to ensure that AI systems do not reinforce negative <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stereotype" title=3D"Stereotype">stereotypes<=
/a> or render certain groups invisible. Procedural fairness focuses on the =
decision process rather than the outcome. The most relevant notions of fair=
ness may depend on the context, notably the type of AI application and the =
stakeholders. The subjectivity in the notions of bias and fairness makes it=
 difficult for companies to operationalize them. Having access to sensitive=
 attributes such as race or gender is also considered by many AI ethicists =
to be necessary in order to compensate for biases, but it may conflict with=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-discrimination_law" title=3D=
"Anti-discrimination law">anti-discrimination laws</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-=
Samuel-2022_251-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Samuel-2022-251"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>241<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>At the 2022 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ACM_Conference_=
on_Fairness,_Accountability,_and_Transparency" title=3D"ACM Conference on F=
airness, Accountability, and Transparency">ACM Conference on Fairness, Acco=
untability, and Transparency</a> a paper reported that a CLIP=E2=80=91based=
 (<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Contrastive_Language-Image_Pre-t=
raining" title=3D"Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training">Contrastive Lang=
uage-Image Pre-training</a>) robotic system reproduced harmful gender=E2=80=
=91 and race=E2=80=91linked stereotypes in a simulated manipulation task. T=
he authors recommended robot=E2=80=91learning methods which physically mani=
fest such harms be "paused, reworked, or even wound down when appropriate, =
until outcomes can be proven safe, effective, and just."<sup id=3D"cite_ref=
-270" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifici=
al_intelligence#cite_note-270"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>257<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-271" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-271"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>258<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-272" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_not=
e-272"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>259<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Lack_of_transparency">Lack =
of transparency</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
See also: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explainable_AI" class=3D=
"mw-redirect" title=3D"Explainable AI">Explainable AI</a>, <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithmic_transparency" title=3D"Algorithmic tra=
nsparency">Algorithmic transparency</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Right_to_explanation" title=3D"Right to explanation">Right to ex=
planation</a></div>
<p>Many AI systems are so complex that their designers cannot explain how t=
hey reach their decisions.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTESample2017_273-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-FOOTNOTESample2017-273"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>260<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Particularly with <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deep_neural_networks" class=3D"mw-red=
irect" title=3D"Deep neural networks">deep neural networks</a>, in which th=
ere are many non-<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear" class=3D"=
mw-redirect" title=3D"Linear">linear</a> relationships between inputs and o=
utputs. But some popular explainability techniques exist.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-274" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-274"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>261<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>It is impossible to be certain that a program is operating correctly=
 if no one knows how exactly it works. There have been many cases where a m=
achine learning program passed rigorous tests, but nevertheless learned som=
ething different than what the programmers intended. For example, a system =
that could identify skin diseases better than medical professionals was fou=
nd to actually have a strong tendency to classify images with a <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruler" title=3D"Ruler">ruler</a> as "cancerou=
s", because pictures of malignancies typically include a ruler to show the =
scale.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChristian2020110_275-0" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_no=
te-FOOTNOTEChristian2020110-275"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>262<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Another machine learning syst=
em designed to help effectively allocate medical resources was found to cla=
ssify patients with asthma as being at "low risk" of dying from pneumonia. =
Having asthma is actually a severe risk factor, but since the patients havi=
ng asthma would usually get much more medical care, they were relatively un=
likely to die according to the training data. The correlation between asthm=
a and low risk of dying from pneumonia was real, but misleading.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChristian202088=E2=80=9391_276-0" class=3D"reference"><a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOT=
NOTEChristian202088%E2%80%9391-276"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>26=
3<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>People who have been harmed by an algorithm's decision have a right =
to an explanation.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-277" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-277"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>264<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup> Doctors, for example, are expected to clearly and completely explain =
to their colleagues the reasoning behind any decision they make. Early draf=
ts of the European Union's <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General=
_Data_Protection_Regulation" title=3D"General Data Protection Regulation">G=
eneral Data Protection Regulation</a> in 2016 included an explicit statemen=
t that this right exists.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-278" class=3D"reference"><a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-278">=
<span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>n<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> Industry experts noted that this is an unsolved problem with no =
solution in sight. Regulators argued that nevertheless the harm is real: if=
 the problem has no solution, the tools should not be used.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-FOOTNOTEChristian202091_279-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChristian202=
091-279"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>265<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DARPA" title=3D"DARPA">DARP=
A</a> established the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explainable_=
Artificial_Intelligence" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Explainable Artific=
ial Intelligence">XAI</a> ("Explainable Artificial Intelligence") program i=
n 2014 to try to solve these problems.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChristian=
202083_280-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChristian202083-280"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>266<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p><p>Several approaches aim to address the transparency problem. SHAP ena=
bles to visualise the contribution of each feature to the output.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEVerma2021_281-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEVerma2021-28=
1"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>267<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup> LIME can locally approximate a model's outputs with a simpl=
er, interpretable model.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERothman2020_282-0" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERothman2020-282"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>268<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multitask_learning" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Mult=
itask learning">Multitask learning</a> provides a large number of outputs i=
n addition to the target classification. These other outputs can help devel=
opers deduce what the network has learned.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChris=
tian2020105=E2=80=93108_283-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChristian2020105=
%E2%80%93108-283"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>269<span class=3D"ci=
te-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dec=
onvolution" title=3D"Deconvolution">Deconvolution</a>, <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeepDream" title=3D"DeepDream">DeepDream</a> and other=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generative_AI" class=3D"mw-redire=
ct" title=3D"Generative AI">generative</a> methods can allow developers to =
see what different layers of a deep network for computer vision have learne=
d, and produce output that can suggest what the network is learning.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChristian2020108=E2=80=93112_284-0" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_not=
e-FOOTNOTEChristian2020108%E2%80%93112-284"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[<=
/span>270<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> For <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generative_pre-trained_transformer" title=3D"Gen=
erative pre-trained transformer">generative pre-trained transformers</a>, <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthropic" title=3D"Anthropic">Anth=
ropic</a> developed a technique based on <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Dictionary_learning" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Dictionary learn=
ing">dictionary learning</a> that associates patterns of neuron activations=
 with human-understandable concepts.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-285" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite=
_note-285"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>271<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Bad_actors_and_weaponized_A=
I">Bad actors and weaponized AI</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lethal_autonomous_w=
eapon" title=3D"Lethal autonomous weapon">Lethal autonomous weapon</a>, <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence_arms_race" ti=
tle=3D"Artificial intelligence arms race">Artificial intelligence arms race=
</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_safety" title=3D"AI s=
afety">AI safety</a></div>
<p>Artificial intelligence provides a number of tools that are useful to <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bad_actor" class=3D"mw-redirect" tit=
le=3D"Bad actor">bad actors</a>, such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Authoritarian" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Authoritarian">authori=
tarian governments</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terrorist"=
 class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Terrorist">terrorists</a>, <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Criminals" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Criminal=
s">criminals</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rogue_states" =
class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Rogue states">rogue states</a>.
</p><p>A lethal autonomous weapon is a machine that locates, selects and en=
gages human targets without human supervision.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-287" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-287"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>o<span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Widely available AI tools can be used by ba=
d actors to develop inexpensive autonomous weapons and, if produced at scal=
e, they are potentially <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weapons_of=
_mass_destruction" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Weapons of mass destructi=
on">weapons of mass destruction</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorv=
ig2021987=E2=80=93990_288-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20219=
87%E2%80%93990-288"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>273<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Even when used in conventional warfare, th=
ey currently cannot reliably choose targets and could potentially <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Murder" title=3D"Murder">kill an innocent=
 person</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021987=E2=80=93990_28=
8-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificia=
l_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021987%E2%80%93990-288"><spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>273<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup> In 2014, 30 nations (including China) supported a ban on autonomou=
s weapons under the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations=
" title=3D"United Nations">United Nations</a>' <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Convention_on_Certain_Conventional_Weapons" title=3D"Conventio=
n on Certain Conventional Weapons">Convention on Certain Conventional Weapo=
ns</a>, however the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States"=
 title=3D"United States">United States</a> and others disagreed.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021988_289-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERu=
ssellNorvig2021988-289"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>274<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> By 2015, over fifty countries were rep=
orted to be researching battlefield robots.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-290" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-290"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>275<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>AI tools make it easier for authoritarian governments to efficiently=
 control their citizens in several ways. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Facial_recognition_system" title=3D"Facial recognition system">Face<=
/a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speaker_recognition" title=
=3D"Speaker recognition">voice recognition</a> allow widespread <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Surveillance" title=3D"Surveillance">surveill=
ance</a>. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_learning" title=
=3D"Machine learning">Machine learning</a>, operating this data, can <a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classifier_(machine_learning)" class=3D"=
mw-redirect" title=3D"Classifier (machine learning)">classify</a> potential=
 enemies of the state and prevent them from hiding. <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Recommendation_systems" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Re=
commendation systems">Recommendation systems</a> can precisely target <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Propaganda" title=3D"Propaganda">propag=
anda</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Misinformation" title=
=3D"Misinformation">misinformation</a> for maximum effect. <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deepfakes" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Deepfake=
s">Deepfakes</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generative_AI=
" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Generative AI">generative AI</a> aid in pr=
oducing misinformation. Advanced AI can make authoritarian <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technocracy" title=3D"Technocracy">centralized dec=
ision-making</a> more competitive than liberal and decentralized systems su=
ch as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Market_(economics)" title=3D=
"Market (economics)">markets</a>. It lowers the cost and difficulty of <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Digital_warfare" class=3D"mw-redirect"=
 title=3D"Digital warfare">digital warfare</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Spyware" title=3D"Spyware">advanced spyware</a>.<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHarari2018_291-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHarari2018-=
291"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>276<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]=
</span></a></sup> All these technologies have been available since 2020 or =
earlier=E2=80=94AI <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facial_recognit=
ion_system" title=3D"Facial recognition system">facial recognition systems<=
/a> are already being used for <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mas=
s_surveillance" title=3D"Mass surveillance">mass surveillance</a> in China.=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-292" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-292"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>277<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-293" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-293"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>2=
78<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>There are many other ways in which AI is expected to help bad actors=
, some of which can not be foreseen. For example, machine-learning AI is ab=
le to design tens of thousands of toxic molecules in a matter of hours.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEUrbinaLentzosInvernizziEkins2022_294-0" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#c=
ite_note-FOOTNOTEUrbinaLentzosInvernizziEkins2022-294"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>279<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Technological_unemployment"=
>Technological unemployment</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workplace_impact_of=
_artificial_intelligence" title=3D"Workplace impact of artificial intellige=
nce">Workplace impact of artificial intelligence</a> and <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_unemployment" title=3D"Technological u=
nemployment">Technological unemployment</a></div>
<p>Economists have frequently highlighted the risks of redundancies from AI=
, and speculated about unemployment if there is no adequate social policy f=
or full employment.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcGaughey2022_295-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcGaughey2022-295"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</s=
pan>280<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In the past, technology has tended to increase rather than reduce to=
tal employment, but economists acknowledge that "we're in uncharted territo=
ry" with AI.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-296" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-296"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>281<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> A survey of economists showed disagreement about whether the increasing u=
se of robots and AI will cause a substantial increase in long-term <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unemployment" title=3D"Unemployment">unem=
ployment</a>, but they generally agree that it could be a net benefit if <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Productivity" title=3D"Productivity"=
>productivity</a> gains are <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redist=
ribution_of_income_and_wealth" title=3D"Redistribution of income and wealth=
">redistributed</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEIGM_Chicago2017_297-0" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEIGM_Chicago2017-297"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>282<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Risk estimates va=
ry; for example, in the 2010s, Michael Osborne and <a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Carl_Benedikt_Frey" title=3D"Carl Benedikt Frey">Carl Bene=
dikt Frey</a> estimated 47% of U.S. jobs are at "high risk" of potential au=
tomation, while an OECD report classified only 9% of U.S. jobs as "high ris=
k".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-299" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-299"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>p<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-300" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A=
rtificial_intelligence#cite_note-300"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
284<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The methodology of specu=
lating about future employment levels has been criticised as lacking eviden=
tial foundation, and for implying that technology, rather than social polic=
y, creates unemployment, as opposed to redundancies.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOO=
TNOTEMcGaughey2022_295-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcGaughey2022-295"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>280<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> In April 2023, it was reported that 70% of the jobs for Chinese =
video game illustrators had been eliminated by generative artificial intell=
igence.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-301" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-301"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>285<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-302" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-302"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[<=
/span>286<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Unlike previous waves of automation, many middle-class jobs may be e=
liminated by artificial intelligence; <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/The_Economist" title=3D"The Economist">The Economist</a></i> stated =
in 2015 that "the worry that AI could do to white-collar jobs what steam po=
wer did to blue-collar ones during the Industrial Revolution" is "worth tak=
ing seriously".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMorgenstern2015_303-0" class=3D"=
reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence=
#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMorgenstern2015-303"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>287<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Jobs at extreme risk r=
ange from <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paralegal" title=3D"Para=
legal">paralegals</a> to fast food cooks, while job demand is likely to inc=
rease for care-related professions ranging from personal healthcare to the =
clergy.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-304" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-304"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>288<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In Ju=
ly 2025, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Motor_Company" title=
=3D"Ford Motor Company">Ford</a> CEO <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Jim_Farley_(businessman)" title=3D"Jim Farley (businessman)">Jim Farley<=
/a> predicted that "artificial intelligence is going to replace literally h=
alf of all <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White-collar_worker" ti=
tle=3D"White-collar worker">white-collar workers</a> in the U.S."<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-305" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-305"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</spa=
n>289<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>From the early days of the development of artificial intelligence, t=
here have been arguments, for example, those put forward by <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Weizenbaum" title=3D"Joseph Weizenbaum">Jo=
seph Weizenbaum</a>, about whether tasks that can be done by computers actu=
ally should be done by them, given the difference between computers and hum=
ans, and between quantitative calculation and qualitative, value-based judg=
ement.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-306" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-306"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>290<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Existential_risk">Existenti=
al risk</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Existential_risk_fro=
m_artificial_intelligence" title=3D"Existential risk from artificial intell=
igence">Existential risk from artificial intelligence</a></div>
<p>Recent public debates in artificial intelligence have increasingly focus=
ed on its broader societal and ethical implications. It has been argued AI =
will become so powerful that humanity may irreversibly lose control of it. =
This could, as physicist <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_H=
awking" title=3D"Stephen Hawking">Stephen Hawking</a> stated, "<a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_catastrophic_risk" title=3D"Global cata=
strophic risk">spell the end of the human race</a>".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOO=
TNOTECellan-Jones2014_307-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTECellan-Jones2014-3=
07"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>291<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup> This scenario has been common in science fiction, when a c=
omputer or robot suddenly develops a human-like "self-awareness" (or "senti=
ence" or "consciousness") and becomes a malevolent character.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-309" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art=
ificial_intelligence#cite_note-309"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>q<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> These sci-fi scenarios are m=
isleading in several ways.
</p><p>First, AI does not require human-like <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Sentience" title=3D"Sentience">sentience</a> to be an existentia=
l risk. Modern AI programs are given specific goals and use learning and in=
telligence to achieve them. Philosopher <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Nick_Bostrom" title=3D"Nick Bostrom">Nick Bostrom</a> argued that if =
one gives <i>almost any</i> goal to a sufficiently powerful AI, it may choo=
se to destroy humanity to achieve it (he used the example of an <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instrumental_convergence#Paperclip_maximizer"=
 title=3D"Instrumental convergence">automated paperclip factory</a> that de=
stroys the world to get more iron for paperclips).<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTN=
OTEBostrom2014_310-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEBostrom2014-310"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>293<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></=
sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_J._Russell" title=3D"S=
tuart J. Russell">Stuart Russell</a> gives the example of household robot t=
hat tries to find a way to kill its owner to prevent it from being unplugge=
d, reasoning that "you can't fetch the coffee if you're dead."<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-FOOTNOTERussell2019_311-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussell2019-3=
11"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>294<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup> In order to be safe for humanity, a <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Superintelligence" title=3D"Superintelligence">superinte=
lligence</a> would have to be genuinely <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/AI_alignment" title=3D"AI alignment">aligned</a> with humanity's mora=
lity and values so that it is "fundamentally on our side".<sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-312" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence#cite_note-312"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>295<s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Second, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yuval_Noah_Harari" =
title=3D"Yuval Noah Harari">Yuval Noah Harari</a> argues that AI does not r=
equire a robot body or physical control to pose an existential risk. The es=
sential parts of civilization are not physical. Things like <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ideologies" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Ideolo=
gies">ideologies</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Law" title=
=3D"Law">law</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government" titl=
e=3D"Government">government</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M=
oney" title=3D"Money">money</a> and the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Economy" title=3D"Economy">economy</a> are built on <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Language" title=3D"Language">language</a>; they exi=
st because there are stories that billions of people believe. The current p=
revalence of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Misinformation" title=
=3D"Misinformation">misinformation</a> suggests that an AI could use langua=
ge to convince people to believe anything, even to take actions that are de=
structive.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHarari2023_313-0" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note=
-FOOTNOTEHarari2023-313"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>296<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Geoffrey Hinton said in 2025 that <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model" title=3D"Large =
language model">modern AI</a> is particularly "good at persuasion" and gett=
ing better all the time. He asks "Suppose you wanted to invade the capital =
of the US. Do you have to go there and do it yourself? No. You just have to=
 be good at persuasion."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEStewart2025_314-0" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStewart2025-314"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>297<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>The opinions amongst experts and industry insiders are mixed, with s=
izable fractions both concerned and unconcerned by risk from eventual super=
intelligent AI.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEM=C3=BCllerBostrom2014_315-0" cl=
ass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intel=
ligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEM%C3%BCllerBostrom2014-315"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>298<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Personal=
ities such as Stephen Hawking, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bil=
l_Gates" title=3D"Bill Gates">Bill Gates</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Elon_Musk" title=3D"Elon Musk">Elon Musk</a>,<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-316" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art=
ificial_intelligence#cite_note-316"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>29=
9<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> as well as AI pioneers suc=
h as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoffrey_Hinton" title=3D"Geo=
ffrey Hinton">Geoffrey Hinton</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Yoshua_Bengio" title=3D"Yoshua Bengio">Yoshua Bengio</a>, <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_J._Russell" title=3D"Stuart J. Russell">Stu=
art Russell</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Demis_Hassabis" t=
itle=3D"Demis Hassabis">Demis Hassabis</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Sam_Altman" title=3D"Sam Altman">Sam Altman</a>, have express=
ed concerns about existential risk from AI.
</p><p>In May 2023, Geoffrey Hinton announced his resignation from Google i=
n order to be able to "freely speak out about the risks of AI" without "con=
sidering how this impacts Google".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-317" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_n=
ote-317"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>300<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup> He notably mentioned risks of an <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_takeover" title=3D"AI takeover">AI takeover</a>,<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-318" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-318"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>301<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and stressed th=
at in order to avoid the worst outcomes, establishing safety guidelines wil=
l require cooperation among those competing in use of AI.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-319" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-319"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>302<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In 2023, many leading AI experts endorsed <a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Statement_on_AI_risk_of_extinction" class=3D"mw-redirect" ti=
tle=3D"Statement on AI risk of extinction">the joint statement</a> that "Mi=
tigating the risk of extinction from AI should be a global priority alongsi=
de other societal-scale risks such as pandemics and nuclear war".<sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEValance2023_320-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEValance202=
3-320"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>303<span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Some other researchers were more optimistic. AI pioneer <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/J%C3%BCrgen_Schmidhuber" title=3D"J=C3=BCrgen =
Schmidhuber">J=C3=BCrgen Schmidhuber</a> did not sign the joint statement, =
emphasising that in 95% of all cases, AI research is about making "human li=
ves longer and healthier and easier."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-321" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cit=
e_note-321"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>304<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup> While the tools that are now being used to improve=
 lives can also be used by bad actors, "they can also be used against the b=
ad actors."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-322" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-322"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>305<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-323" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-323"><span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">[</span>306<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andrew_Ng" title=3D"Andrew Ng">Andrew Ng</a> als=
o argued that "it's a mistake to fall for the doomsday hype on AI=E2=80=94a=
nd that regulators who do will only benefit vested interests."<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-324" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-324"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3=
07<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Yann_LeCun" title=3D"Yann LeCun">Yann LeCun</a>, a Turing A=
ward winner, disagreed with the idea that AI will subordinate humans "simpl=
y because they are smarter, let alone destroy [us]",<sup id=3D"cite_ref-325=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_i=
ntelligence#cite_note-325"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>308<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> "scoff[ing] at his peers' dystopian=
 scenarios of supercharged misinformation and even, eventually, human extin=
ction."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-326" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-326"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>309<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In th=
e early 2010s, experts argued that the risks are too distant in the future =
to warrant research or that humans will be valuable from the perspective of=
 a superintelligent machine.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-327" class=3D"reference"><a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-32=
7"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>310<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup> However, after 2016, the study of current and future risks =
and possible solutions became a serious area of research.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-FOOTNOTEChristian202067,_73_328-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChristian2=
02067,_73-328"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>311<span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Ethical_machines_and_alignm=
ent">Ethical machines and alignment</h3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_ethics" tit=
le=3D"Machine ethics">Machine ethics</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/AI_safety" title=3D"AI safety">AI safety</a>, <a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friendly_artificial_intelligence" title=3D"Friendly art=
ificial intelligence">Friendly artificial intelligence</a>, <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_moral_agents" class=3D"mw-redirect" ti=
tle=3D"Artificial moral agents">Artificial moral agents</a>, and <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Compatible" title=3D"Human Compatible"=
>Human Compatible</a></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
See also: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human-AI_interaction" ti=
tle=3D"Human-AI interaction">Human-AI interaction</a></div>
<p>Friendly AI are machines that have been designed from the beginning to m=
inimize risks and to make choices that benefit humans. <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eliezer_Yudkowsky" title=3D"Eliezer Yudkowsky">Eliezer=
 Yudkowsky</a>, who coined the term, argues that developing friendly AI sho=
uld be a higher research priority: it may require a large investment and it=
 must be completed before AI becomes an existential risk.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-FOOTNOTEYudkowsky2008_329-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wi=
kipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEYudkowsky2008-32=
9"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>312<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup>
</p><p>Machines with intelligence have the potential to use their intellige=
nce to make ethical decisions. The field of machine ethics provides machine=
s with ethical principles and procedures for resolving ethical dilemmas.<su=
p id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAndersonAnderson2011_330-0" class=3D"reference"><a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FO=
OTNOTEAndersonAnderson2011-330"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>313<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
The field of machine ethics is also called computational morality,<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAndersonAnderson2011_330-1" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNO=
TEAndersonAnderson2011-330"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>313<span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
and was founded at an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AAAI" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"AAAI">AAAI</a> symposium in 2005.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-FOOTNOTEAAAI2014_331-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAAAI2014-331"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>314<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a=
></sup>
</p><p>Other approaches include <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/We=
ndell_Wallach" title=3D"Wendell Wallach">Wendell Wallach</a>'s "artificial =
moral agents"<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEWallach2010_332-0" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_=
note-FOOTNOTEWallach2010-332"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>315<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Stuart_J._Russell" title=3D"Stuart J. Russell">Stuart J. Russe=
ll</a>'s <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Compatible#Russell'=
s_three_principles" title=3D"Human Compatible">three principles</a> for dev=
eloping provably beneficial machines.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussell201=
9173_333-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussell2019173-333"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>316<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Open_source">Open source</h=
3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
See also: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open-source_artificial_i=
ntelligence" title=3D"Open-source artificial intelligence">Open-source arti=
ficial intelligence</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lists_=
of_open-source_artificial_intelligence_software" title=3D"Lists of open-sou=
rce artificial intelligence software">Lists of open-source artificial intel=
ligence software</a></div>
<p>Active organizations in the AI open-source community include <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugging_Face" title=3D"Hugging Face">Hugging =
Face</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-334" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-334"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>317<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google" title=3D"Google">Google</a>,<=
sup id=3D"cite_ref-335" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-335"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>318<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EleutherAI" title=3D"EleutherAI">EleutherAI</a>=
 and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta_Platforms" title=3D"Meta=
 Platforms">Meta</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-336" class=3D"reference"><a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-336"><s=
pan class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>319<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span>=
</a></sup> Various AI models, such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/LLaMA" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"LLaMA">Llama 2</a>, <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mistral_AI" title=3D"Mistral AI">Mistral</a> or=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stable_Diffusion" title=3D"Stable=
 Diffusion">Stable Diffusion</a>, have been made open-weight,<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-337" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art=
ificial_intelligence#cite_note-337"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>32=
0<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-338" cl=
ass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intel=
ligence#cite_note-338"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>321<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> meaning that their architecture and tr=
ained parameters (the "weights") are publicly available. Open-weight models=
 can be freely <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fine-tuning_(deep_l=
earning)" title=3D"Fine-tuning (deep learning)">fine-tuned</a>, which allow=
s companies to specialize them with their own data and for their own use-ca=
se.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-339" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-339"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>322<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Open-weig=
ht models are useful for research and innovation but can also be misused. S=
ince they can be fine-tuned, any built-in security measure, such as objecti=
ng to harmful requests, can be trained away until it becomes ineffective. S=
ome researchers warn that future AI models may develop dangerous capabiliti=
es (such as the potential to drastically facilitate <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Bioterrorism" title=3D"Bioterrorism">bioterrorism</a>) an=
d that once released on the Internet, they cannot be deleted everywhere if =
needed. They recommend pre-release audits and cost-benefit analyses.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-340" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-340"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>323<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Frameworks">Frameworks</h3>=
</div>
<p>Artificial intelligence projects can be guided by ethical considerations=
 during the design, development, and implementation of an AI system. An AI =
framework such as the Care and Act Framework, developed by the <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Turing_Institute" title=3D"Alan Turing In=
stitute">Alan Turing Institute</a> and based on the SUM values, outlines fo=
ur main ethical dimensions, defined as follows:<sup id=3D"cite_ref-341" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-341"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>324<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-342" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite=
_note-342"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>325<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<ul><li><b>Respect</b> the dignity of individual people</li>
<li><b>Connect</b> with other people sincerely, openly, and inclusively</li=
>
<li><b>Care</b> for the wellbeing of everyone</li>
<li><b>Protect</b> social values, justice, and the public interest</li></ul=
>
<p>Other developments in ethical frameworks include those decided upon duri=
ng the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asilomar_Conference_on_Bene=
ficial_AI" title=3D"Asilomar Conference on Beneficial AI">Asilomar Conferen=
ce</a>, the Montreal Declaration for Responsible AI, and the IEEE's Ethics =
of Autonomous Systems initiative, among others;<sup id=3D"cite_ref-343" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-343"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>326<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> however, these principles are not with=
out criticism, especially regarding the people chosen to contribute to thes=
e frameworks.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-344" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-344"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>327<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p><p>Promotion of the wellbeing of the people and communities that these =
technologies affect requires consideration of the social and ethical implic=
ations at all stages of AI system design, development and implementation, a=
nd collaboration between job roles such as data scientists, product manager=
s, data engineers, domain experts, and delivery managers.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-345" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-345"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>328<sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>The <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_Safety_Institute_(Un=
ited_Kingdom)" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"AI Safety Institute (United K=
ingdom)">UK AI Safety Institute</a> released in 2024 a testing toolset call=
ed 'Inspect' for AI safety evaluations available under an MIT open-source l=
icence which is freely available on GitHub and can be improved with third-p=
arty packages. It can be used to evaluate AI models in a range of areas inc=
luding core knowledge, ability to reason, and autonomous capabilities.<sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-346" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-346"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>329<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
