"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-Dartmouth_workshop_8-1=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_i=
ntelligence#cite_note-Dartmouth_workshop-8"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[<=
/span>6<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The attendees became=
 the leaders of AI research in the 1960s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-375" class=3D"=
reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence=
#cite_note-375"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>t<span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">]</span></a></sup> They and their students produced programs that t=
he press described as "astonishing":<sup id=3D"cite_ref-377" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite=
_note-377"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>u<span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">]</span></a></sup> computers were learning <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Checkers" title=3D"Checkers">checkers</a> strategies, solving w=
ord problems in algebra, proving <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/T=
heorem" title=3D"Theorem">logical theorems</a> and speaking English.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-378" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/w=
iki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-378"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</=
span>v<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-Su=
cc1_9-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artif=
icial_intelligence#cite_note-Succ1-9"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>=
7<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Artificial intelligence la=
boratories were set up at a number of British and U.S. universities in the =
latter 1950s and early 1960s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Clarendon_Press-2004_367-2=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_i=
ntelligence#cite_note-Clarendon_Press-2004-367"><span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">[</span>350<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Researchers in the 1960s and the 1970s were convinced that their met=
hods would eventually succeed in creating a machine with <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_general_intelligence" title=3D"Artificial=
 general intelligence">general intelligence</a> and considered this the goa=
l of their field.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTENewquist199486=E2=80=9386_379-=
0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_=
intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTENewquist199486%E2%80%9386-379"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>357<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> In 1965 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herbert_A._Simon" title=
=3D"Herbert A. Simon">Herbert Simon</a> predicted, "machines will be capabl=
e, within twenty years, of doing any work a man can do".<sup id=3D"cite_ref=
-380" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifici=
al_intelligence#cite_note-380"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>358<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In 1967 <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Marvin_Minsky" title=3D"Marvin Minsky">Marvin Minsky</a> =
agreed, writing that "within a generation&nbsp;... the problem of creating =
'artificial intelligence' will substantially be solved".<sup id=3D"cite_ref=
-381" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifici=
al_intelligence#cite_note-381"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>359<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> They had, however, underestimat=
ed the difficulty of the problem.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-383" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_no=
te-383"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>w<span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
]</span></a></sup> In 1974, both the U.S. and British governments cut off e=
xploratory research in response to the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Lighthill_report" title=3D"Lighthill report">criticism</a> of <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sir_James_Lighthill" class=3D"mw-redirect=
" title=3D"Sir James Lighthill">Sir James Lighthill</a><sup id=3D"cite_ref-=
FOOTNOTELighthill1973_384-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTELighthill1973-384"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>361<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup> and ongoing pressure from the U.S. Congress to <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansfield_Amendment" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"Mansfield Amendment">fund more productive projects</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-FOOTNOTENRC1999212=E2=80=93213_385-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTENRC19=
99212%E2%80%93213-385"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>362<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Marvin_Minsky" title=3D"Marvin Minsky">Minsky</a> and <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Papert" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Papert">Paper=
t</a>'s book <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perceptron" title=
=3D"Perceptron">Perceptrons</a></i> was understood as proving that <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_neural_networks" class=3D"mw-r=
edirect" title=3D"Artificial neural networks">artificial neural networks</a=
> would never be useful for solving real-world tasks, thus discrediting the=
 approach altogether.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig202122_386-0"=
 class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_in=
telligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig202122-386"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>363<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The "<a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_winter" title=3D"AI winter">AI win=
ter</a>", a period when obtaining funding for AI projects was difficult, fo=
llowed.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-First_AI_Winter_11-1" class=3D"reference"><a hre=
f=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-First_=
AI_Winter-11"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>9<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In the early 1980s, AI research was revived by the commercial succes=
s of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expert_system" title=3D"Exper=
t system">expert systems</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-387" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-3=
87"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>364<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup> a form of AI program that simulated the knowledge and anal=
ytical skills of human experts. By 1985, the market for AI had reached over=
 a billion dollars. At the same time, Japan's <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Fifth_generation_computer" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Fifth=
 generation computer">fifth generation computer</a> project inspired the U.=
S. and British governments to restore funding for <a href=3D"https://en.wik=
ipedia.org/wiki/Academic_research" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Academic =
research">academic research</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Fund01_10-1" class=3D"r=
eference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#=
cite_note-Fund01-10"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>8<span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> However, beginning with the collapse of the=
 <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lisp_Machine" class=3D"mw-redirec=
t" title=3D"Lisp Machine">Lisp Machine</a> market in 1987, AI once again fe=
ll into disrepute, and a second, longer-lasting winter began.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-Second_AI_Winter_12-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wiki=
pedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Second_AI_Winter-12"><span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>10<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup>
</p><p>Up to this point, most of AI's funding had gone to projects that use=
d high-level <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbolic_AI" class=3D=
"mw-redirect" title=3D"Symbolic AI">symbols</a> to represent <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mental_objects" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"M=
ental objects">mental objects</a> like plans, goals, beliefs, and known fac=
ts. In the 1980s, some researchers began to doubt that this approach would =
be able to imitate all the processes of human cognition, especially <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Machine_perception" title=3D"Machine perc=
eption">perception</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robotics" =
title=3D"Robotics">robotics</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M=
achine_learning" title=3D"Machine learning">learning</a> and <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pattern_recognition" title=3D"Pattern recognitio=
n">pattern recognition</a>,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig202124_=
388-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig202124-388"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>365<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> an=
d began to look into "sub-symbolic" approaches.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTE=
Nilsson19987_389-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTENilsson19987-389"><span cla=
ss=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>366<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rodney_Brooks" title=3D"Rodney=
 Brooks">Rodney Brooks</a> rejected "representation" in general and focusse=
d directly on engineering machines that move and survive.<sup id=3D"cite_re=
f-394" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-394"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>x<span=
 class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Judea_Pearl" title=3D"Judea Pearl">Judea Pearl</a>, <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lotfi_Zadeh" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Lotf=
i Zadeh">Lotfi Zadeh</a>, and others developed methods that handled incompl=
ete and uncertain information by making reasonable guesses rather than prec=
ise logic.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Stoch_93-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Stoch-93"><sp=
an class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>87<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></=
a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig202125_395-0" class=3D"ref=
erence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#ci=
te_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig202125-395"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>371<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> But the most importan=
t development was the revival of "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Connectionism" title=3D"Connectionism">connectionism</a>", including neural=
 network research, by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geoffrey_Hin=
ton" title=3D"Geoffrey Hinton">Geoffrey Hinton</a> and others.<sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-396" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-396"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3=
72<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> In 1990, <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yann_LeCun" title=3D"Yann LeCun">Yann LeCun</a> su=
ccessfully showed that <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convolution=
al_neural_networks" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Convolutional neural net=
works">convolutional neural networks</a> can recognize handwritten digits, =
