<p>In 2016, issues of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithmic_=
fairness" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Algorithmic fairness">fairness</a>=
 and the misuse of technology were catapulted into center stage at machine =
learning conferences, publications vastly increased, funding became availab=
le, and many researchers re-focussed their careers on these issues. The <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AI_alignment" title=3D"AI alignment">=
alignment problem</a> became a serious field of academic study.<sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-FOOTNOTEChristian202067,_73_328-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChri=
stian202067,_73-328"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>311<span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>In the late 2010s and early 2020s, AGI companies began to deliver pr=
ograms that created enormous interest. In 2015, <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/AlphaGo" title=3D"AlphaGo">AlphaGo</a>, developed by <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DeepMind" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"=
DeepMind">DeepMind</a>, beat the world champion <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Go_player" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Go player">Go playe=
r</a>. The program taught only the game's rules and developed a strategy by=
 itself. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GPT-3" title=3D"GPT-3">GP=
T-3</a> is a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model"=
 title=3D"Large language model">large language model</a> that was released =
in 2020 by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenAI" title=3D"OpenAI=
">OpenAI</a> and is capable of generating high-quality human-like text.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-406" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-406"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>380<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChatGPT" title=3D"ChatGPT">ChatGPT</a>, launched o=
n 30 November 2022, became the fastest-growing consumer software applicatio=
n in history, gaining over 100 million users in two months.<sup id=3D"cite_=
ref-407" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artif=
icial_intelligence#cite_note-407"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>381<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> It marked what is widely reg=
arded as AI's breakout year, bringing it into the public consciousness.<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-408" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-408"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>382<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> These programs, =
and others, inspired an aggressive <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/AI_boom" title=3D"AI boom">AI boom</a>, where large companies began invest=
ing billions of dollars in AI research. According to AI Impacts, about US$5=
0 billion annually was invested in "AI" around 2022 in the U.S. alone and a=
bout 20% of the new U.S. Computer Science PhD graduates have specialized in=
 "AI".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDiFeliciantonio2023_409-0" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite=
_note-FOOTNOTEDiFeliciantonio2023-409"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>383<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> About 800,000 "AI"-rela=
ted U.S. job openings existed in 2022.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEGoswami20=
23_410-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arti=
ficial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGoswami2023-410"><span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">[</span>384<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Accordi=
ng to PitchBook research, 22% of newly funded <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Startups" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Startups">startups</a>=
 in 2024 claimed to be AI companies.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-411" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite=
_note-411"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>385<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Philosophy">Philosophy</h2>=
</div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy_of_artifi=
cial_intelligence" title=3D"Philosophy of artificial intelligence">Philosop=
hy of artificial intelligence</a></div>
<p>Philosophical debates have historically sought to determine the nature o=
f intelligence and how to make intelligent machines.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-412=
" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_i=
ntelligence#cite_note-412"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>386<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Another major focus has been whethe=
r machines can be conscious, and the associated ethical implications.<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-Jarow-2024_413-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Jarow-2024-413"><span c=
lass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>387<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a><=
/sup> Many other topics in philosophy are relevant to AI, such as <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epistemology" title=3D"Epistemology">epis=
temology</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Free_will" title=
=3D"Free will">free will</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-414" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-4=
14"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>388<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]<=
/span></a></sup> Rapid advancements have intensified public discussions on =
the philosophy and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethics_of_AI" c=
lass=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Ethics of AI">ethics of AI</a>.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-Jarow-2024_413-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia=
.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Jarow-2024-413"><span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">[</span>387<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Defining_artificial_intelli=
gence">Defining artificial intelligence</h3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
See also: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Synthetic_intelligence" =
title=3D"Synthetic intelligence">Synthetic intelligence</a>, <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligent_agent" title=3D"Intelligent agent">I=
ntelligent agent</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_m=
ind_(disambiguation)" class=3D"mw-redirect mw-disambig" title=3D"Artificial=
 mind (disambiguation)">Artificial mind</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Virtual_intelligence" title=3D"Virtual intelligence">Virtual int=
elligence</a>, and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dartmouth_works=
hop" title=3D"Dartmouth workshop">Dartmouth workshop</a></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Turing" title=3D"Alan Turi=
ng">Alan Turing</a> wrote in 1950 "I propose to consider the question 'can =
machines think'?"<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTETuring19501_415-0" class=3D"re=
ference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#c=
ite_note-FOOTNOTETuring19501-415"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>389<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> He advised changing the ques=
tion from whether a machine "thinks", to "whether or not it is possible for=
 machinery to show intelligent behaviour".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTETurin=
g19501_415-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTETuring19501-415"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>389<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> He =
devised the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turing_test" title=3D"=
Turing test">Turing test</a>, which measures the ability of a machine to si=
mulate human conversation.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Turing_371-1" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_=
note-Turing-371"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>353<span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Since we can only observe the behavior of the=
 machine, it does not matter if it is "actually" thinking or literally has =
a "mind". Turing notes that <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proble=
m_of_other_minds" title=3D"Problem of other minds">we can not determine the=
se things about other people</a> but "it is usual to have a polite conventi=
on that everyone thinks."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTETuring1950Under_&quot;=
The_Argument_from_Consciousness&quot;_416-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTETu=
ring1950Under_%22The_Argument_from_Consciousness%22-416"><span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">[</span>390<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Weakness_of_Turing_test_1.svg" class=3D"mw-fi=
le-description"><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/=
thumb/5/57/Weakness_of_Turing_test_1.svg/250px-Weakness_of_Turing_test_1.sv=
g.png" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"250" height=3D"182" class=3D"mw-file-ele=
ment" data-file-width=3D"433" data-file-height=3D"316"></a><figcaption>The =
Turing test can provide some evidence of intelligence, but it penalizes non=
-human intelligent behavior.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-417" class=3D"reference"><a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-41=
7"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>391<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</=
span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_J._Russell" title=3D"Stu=
art J. Russell">Russell</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No=
rvig" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Norvig">Norvig</a> agree with Turing t=
hat intelligence must be defined in terms of external behavior, not interna=
l structure.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20211=E2=80=934_1-1" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20211%E2%80%934-1"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>1<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Howev=
er, they are critical that the test requires the machine to imitate humans.=
 "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeronautics" title=3D"Aeronautic=
s">Aeronautical engineering</a> texts", they wrote, "do not define the goal=
 of their field as making 'machines that fly so exactly like <a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pigeon" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Pigeon">p=
igeons</a> that they can fool other pigeons.<span style=3D"padding-right:.1=
5em;">'</span>"<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20213_418-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20213-418"><span class=3D"cite-bracket"=
>[</span>392<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> AI founder <a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_McCarthy_(computer_scientist)" ti=
tle=3D"John McCarthy (computer scientist)">John McCarthy</a> agreed, writin=
g that "Artificial intelligence is not, by definition, simulation of human =
intelligence".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMaker2006_419-0" class=3D"referen=
ce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_n=
ote-FOOTNOTEMaker2006-419"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>393<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>McCarthy defines intelligence as "the computational part of the abil=
ity to achieve goals in the world".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcCarthy1999=
_420-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcCarthy1999-420"><span class=3D"cite-b=
racket">[</span>394<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Another =
AI founder, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvin_Minsky" title=
=3D"Marvin Minsky">Marvin Minsky</a>, similarly describes it as "the abilit=
y to solve hard problems".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMinsky1986_421-0" cla=
ss=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intell=
igence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMinsky1986-421"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>395<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <i><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_Intelligence:_A_Modern_Approach" title=3D=
"Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach">Artificial Intelligence: A Mod=
ern Approach</a></i> defines it as the study of agents that perceive their =
environment and take actions that maximize their chances of achieving defin=
ed goals.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20211=E2=80=934_1-2" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20211%E2%80%934-1"><span class=3D"cite=
-bracket">[</span>1<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>=20
</p><p>The many differing definitiuons of AI have been critically analyzed.=
<sup id=3D"cite_ref-422" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-422"><span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">[</span>396<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"ci=
te_ref-423" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ar=
tificial_intelligence#cite_note-423"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>3=
97<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-424" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-424"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>398<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> During the 2020s AI boom, the term has=
 been used as a marketing <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buzzword=
" title=3D"Buzzword">buzzword</a> to promote products and services which do=
 not use AI.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-425" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-425"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>399<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Legal_definitions">Legal de=
finitions</h4></div>
<p>The <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_Organization_=
for_Standardization" title=3D"International Organization for Standardizatio=
n">International Organization for Standardization</a> describes an AI syste=
m as a "an engineered system that generates outputs such as content, foreca=
sts, recommendations, or decisions for a given set of human=E2=80=91defined=
 objectives, and can operate with varying levels of automation".<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-426" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-426"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>400<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> The <a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_Intelligence_Act" title=3D"Artificial Inte=
lligence Act">EU AI Act</a> defines an AI system as "a machine-based system=
 that is designed to operate with varying levels of autonomy and that may e=
xhibit adaptiveness after deployment, and that, for explicit or implicit ob=
jectives, infers, from the input it receives, how to generate outputs such =
as predictions, content, recommendations, or decisions that can influence p=
hysical or virtual environments".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-427" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_no=
te-427"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>401<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup> In the United States, influential but non=E2=80=91bind=
ing guidance such as the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_=
Institute_of_Standards_and_Technology" title=3D"National Institute of Stand=
ards and Technology">National Institute of Standards and Technology</a>'s A=
I Risk Management Framework describes an AI system as "an engineered or mac=
hine-based system that can, for a given set of objectives, generate outputs=
 such as predictions, recommendations, or decisions influencing real or vir=
tual environments. AI systems are designed to operate with varying levels o=
f autonomy".<sup id=3D"cite_ref-428" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-428"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>402<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Evaluating_approaches_to_AI=
">Evaluating approaches to AI</h3></div>
<p>No established unifying theory or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/Paradigm" title=3D"Paradigm">paradigm</a> has guided AI research for mos=
t of its history.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-430" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-430"><span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>aa<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></s=
up> The unprecedented success of statistical machine learning in the 2010s =
eclipsed all other approaches (so much so that some sources, especially in =
the business world, use the term "artificial intelligence" to mean "machine=
 learning with neural networks"). This approach is mostly <a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sub-symbolic" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Sub-sy=
mbolic">sub-symbolic</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soft_com=
puting" title=3D"Soft computing">soft</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikiped=
ia.org/wiki/Artificial_general_intelligence" title=3D"Artificial general in=
telligence">narrow</a>. Critics argue that these questions may have to be r=
evisited by future generations of AI researchers.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Symbolic_AI_and_its_limits"=
>Symbolic AI and its limits</h4></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Symbolic_AI" class=3D"mw-redire=
ct" title=3D"Symbolic AI">Symbolic AI</a> (or "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/GOFAI" title=3D"GOFAI">GOFAI</a>")<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTE=
Haugeland1985112=E2=80=93117_431-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEHaugeland19=
85112%E2%80%93117-431"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>404<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> simulated the high-level conscious rea=
soning that people use when they solve puzzles, express legal reasoning and=
 do mathematics. They were highly successful at "intelligent" tasks such as=
 algebra or IQ tests. In the 1960s, Newell and Simon proposed the <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Physical_symbol_systems_hypothesis" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Physical symbol systems hypothesis">physical symb=
ol systems hypothesis</a>: "A physical symbol system has the necessary and =
sufficient means of general intelligent action."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-432" cl=
ass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intel=
ligence#cite_note-432"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>405<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>However, the symbolic approach failed on many tasks that humans solv=
e easily, such as learning, recognizing an object or <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Commonsense_reasoning" title=3D"Commonsense reasoning">c=
ommonsense reasoning</a>. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moravec%=
27s_paradox" title=3D"Moravec's paradox">Moravec's paradox</a> is the disco=
very that high-level "intelligent" tasks were easy for AI, but low level "i=
nstinctive" tasks were extremely difficult.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-433" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-433"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>406<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Philosopher <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedi=
a.org/wiki/Hubert_Dreyfus" title=3D"Hubert Dreyfus">Hubert Dreyfus</a> had =
<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dreyfus%27_critique_of_AI" class=
=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Dreyfus' critique of AI">argued</a> since the 196=
0s that human expertise depends on unconscious instinct rather than conscio=
us symbol manipulation, and on having a "feel" for the situation, rather th=
an explicit symbolic knowledge.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-434" class=3D"reference"=
><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note=
-434"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>407<span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
]</span></a></sup> Although his arguments had been ridiculed and ignored wh=
en they were first presented, eventually, AI research came to agree with hi=
m.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-436" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-436"><span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">[</span>ab<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"c=
ite_ref-Psychological_evidence_of_the_prevalence_of_sub_18-2" class=3D"refe=
rence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cit=
e_note-Psychological_evidence_of_the_prevalence_of_sub-18"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>16<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>The issue is not resolved: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Sub-symbolic" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Sub-symbolic">sub-symbolic</a>=
 reasoning can make many of the same inscrutable mistakes that human intuit=
ion does, such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Algorithmic_bias=
" title=3D"Algorithmic bias">algorithmic bias</a>. Critics such as <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noam_Chomsky" title=3D"Noam Chomsky">Noam=
 Chomsky</a> argue continuing research into symbolic AI will still be neces=
sary to attain general intelligence,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTELangley2011=
_437-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTELangley2011-437"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>409<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D=
"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEKatz2012_438-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEKatz2012-438"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>410<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup> in part because sub-symbolic AI is a move away from <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Explainable_AI" class=3D"mw-redirect" tit=
le=3D"Explainable AI">explainable AI</a>: it can be difficult or impossible=
 to understand why a modern statistical AI program made a particular decisi=
on. The emerging field of <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neuro-sy=
mbolic_AI" title=3D"Neuro-symbolic AI">neuro-symbolic artificial intelligen=
ce</a> attempts to bridge the two approaches.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Neat_vs._scruffy">Neat vs. =
scruffy</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neats_and_scruffies"=
 title=3D"Neats and scruffies">Neats and scruffies</a></div>
<p>"Neats" hope that intelligent behavior is described using simple, elegan=
t principles (such as <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Logic" title=
=3D"Logic">logic</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optimization=
" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Optimization">optimization</a>, or <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_neural_network" class=3D"mw-re=
direct" title=3D"Artificial neural network">neural networks</a>). "Scruffie=
s" expect that it necessarily requires solving a large number of unrelated =
problems. Neats defend their programs with theoretical rigor, scruffies rel=
y mainly on incremental testing to see if they work. This issue was activel=
y discussed in the 1970s and 1980s,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-439" class=3D"refere=
nce"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_=
note-439"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>411<span class=3D"cite-brack=
et">]</span></a></sup> but eventually was seen as irrelevant. Modern AI has=
 elements of both.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Soft_vs._hard_computing">So=
ft vs. hard computing</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soft_computing" titl=
e=3D"Soft computing">Soft computing</a></div>
<p>Finding a provably correct or optimal solution is <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Intractability_(complexity)" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=
=3D"Intractability (complexity)">intractable</a> for many important problem=
s.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Intractability_and_efficiency_and_the_combinatorial_e=
xplosion_17-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki=
/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Intractability_and_efficiency_and_the_co=
mbinatorial_explosion-17"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>15<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Soft computing is a set of techniques=
, including <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_algorithms" cl=
ass=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Genetic algorithms">genetic algorithms</a>, <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuzzy_logic" title=3D"Fuzzy logic">f=
uzzy logic</a> and neural networks, that are tolerant of imprecision, uncer=
tainty, partial truth and approximation. Soft computing was introduced in t=
he late 1980s and most successful AI programs in the 21st century are examp=
les of soft computing with neural networks.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Narrow_vs._general_AI">Narr=
ow vs. general AI</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Weak_artificial_int=
elligence" title=3D"Weak artificial intelligence">Weak artificial intellige=
nce</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_general_int=
elligence" title=3D"Artificial general intelligence">Artificial general int=
elligence</a></div>
<p>AI researchers are divided as to whether to pursue the goals of artifici=
al general intelligence and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superi=
ntelligence" title=3D"Superintelligence">superintelligence</a> directly or =
to solve as many specific problems as possible (narrow AI) in hopes these s=
olutions will lead indirectly to the field's long-term goals.<sup id=3D"cit=
e_ref-FOOTNOTEPennachinGoertzel2007_440-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEPenn=
achinGoertzel2007-440"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>412<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERoberts2016=
_441-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artifi=
cial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERoberts2016-441"><span class=3D"cite-br=
acket">[</span>413<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> General i=
ntelligence is difficult to define and difficult to measure, and modern AI =
has had more verifiable successes by focusing on specific problems with spe=
cific solutions. The sub-field of artificial general intelligence studies t=
his area exclusively.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Machine_consciousness,_sent=
ience,_and_mind"><span id=3D"Machine_consciousness.2C_sentience.2C_and_mind=
"></span>Machine consciousness, sentience, and mind</h3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy_of_artif=
icial_intelligence" title=3D"Philosophy of artificial intelligence">Philoso=
phy of artificial intelligence</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_consciousness" title=3D"Artificial consciousness">Artificia=
l consciousness</a></div>
<p>There is no settled consensus in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wik=
i/Philosophy_of_mind" title=3D"Philosophy of mind">philosophy of mind</a> o=
n whether a machine can have a <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Min=
d" title=3D"Mind">mind</a>, <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consci=
ousness" title=3D"Consciousness">consciousness</a> and <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philosophy_of_mind" title=3D"Philosophy of mind">menta=
l states</a> in the same sense that human beings do. This issue considers t=
he internal experiences of the machine, rather than its external behavior. =
Mainstream AI research considers this issue irrelevant because it does not =
affect the goals of the field: to build machines that can solve problems us=
ing intelligence. <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuart_J._Russel=
l" title=3D"Stuart J. Russell">Russell</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipe=
dia.org/wiki/Norvig" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Norvig">Norvig</a> add =
that "[t]he additional project of making a machine conscious in exactly the=
 way humans are is not one that we are equipped to take on."<sup id=3D"cite=
_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig2021986_442-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTERussel=
lNorvig2021986-442"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>414<span class=3D"=
cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> However, the question has become central t=
o the philosophy of mind. It is also typically the central question at issu=
e in <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence_in_fi=
ction" title=3D"Artificial intelligence in fiction">artificial intelligence=
 in fiction</a>.
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Consciousness">Consciousnes=
s</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hard_problem_of_con=
sciousness" title=3D"Hard problem of consciousness">Hard problem of conscio=
usness</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theory_of_mind" tit=
le=3D"Theory of mind">Theory of mind</a></div>
<p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Chalmers" title=3D"David =
Chalmers">David Chalmers</a> identified two problems in understanding the m=
ind, which he named the "hard" and "easy" problems of consciousness.<sup id=
=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEChalmers1995_443-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"htt=
ps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChalme=
rs1995-443"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>415<span class=3D"cite-bra=
cket">]</span></a></sup> The easy problem is understanding how the brain pr=
ocesses signals, makes plans and controls behavior. The hard problem is exp=
laining how this <i>feels</i> or why it should feel like anything at all, a=
ssuming we are right in thinking that it truly does feel like something (De=
nnett's consciousness illusionism says this is an illusion). While human <a=
 href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Information_processing_(psychology)"=
 title=3D"Information processing (psychology)">information processing</a> i=
s easy to explain, human <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subjectiv=
e_experience" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Subjective experience">subject=
ive experience</a> is difficult to explain. For example, it is easy to imag=
ine a color-blind person who has learned to identify which objects in their=
 field of view are red, but it is not clear what would be required for the =
person to <i>know what red looks like</i>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEDenne=
tt1991_444-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEDennett1991-444"><span class=3D"c=
ite-bracket">[</span>416<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"Computationalism_and_functi=
onalism">Computationalism and functionalism</h4></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main articles: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computational_theor=
y_of_mind" title=3D"Computational theory of mind">Computational theory of m=
ind</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Functionalism_(philoso=
phy_of_mind)" title=3D"Functionalism (philosophy of mind)">Functionalism (p=
hilosophy of mind)</a></div>
<p>Computationalism is the position in the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Philosophy_of_mind" title=3D"Philosophy of mind">philosophy of min=
d</a> that the human mind is an information processing system and that thin=
king is a form of computing. Computationalism argues that the relationship =
between mind and body is similar or identical to the relationship between s=
oftware and hardware and thus may be a solution to the <a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mind%E2%80%93body_problem" title=3D"Mind=E2=80=93body =
problem">mind=E2=80=93body problem</a>. This philosophical position was ins=
pired by the work of AI researchers and cognitive scientists in the 1960s a=
nd was originally proposed by philosophers <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Jerry_Fodor" title=3D"Jerry Fodor">Jerry Fodor</a> and <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hilary_Putnam" title=3D"Hilary Putnam">Hilary=
 Putnam</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEHorst2005_445-0" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_not=
e-FOOTNOTEHorst2005-445"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>417<span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Philosopher <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Searle" ti=
tle=3D"John Searle">John Searle</a> characterized this position as "<a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strong_AI_hypothesis" class=3D"mw-redirec=
t" title=3D"Strong AI hypothesis">strong AI</a>": "The appropriately progra=
mmed computer with the right inputs and outputs would thereby have a mind i=
n exactly the same sense human beings have minds."<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Searl=
e's_strong_AI_449-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Searle's_strong_AI-449"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>ac<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
> Searle challenges this claim with his <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Chinese_room" title=3D"Chinese room">Chinese room</a> argument, which=
 attempts to show that even a computer capable of perfectly simulating huma=
n behavior would not have a mind.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-450" class=3D"referenc=
e"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_no=
te-450"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>421<span class=3D"cite-bracket=
">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading4"><h4 id=3D"AI_welfare_and_rights">AI w=
elfare and rights</h4></div>
<p>It is difficult or impossible to reliably evaluate whether an advanced <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentient_AI" class=3D"mw-redirect" =
title=3D"Sentient AI">AI is sentient</a> (has the ability to feel), and if =
so, to what degree.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-451" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-451"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>422<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup> But if there is a significant chance that a given machine can feel a=
nd suffer, then it may be entitled to certain rights or welfare protection =
measures, similarly to animals.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Thomson-2022_452-0" clas=
s=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelli=
gence#cite_note-Thomson-2022-452"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>423<=
span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-Kateman-2=
023_453-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Art=
ificial_intelligence#cite_note-Kateman-2023-453"><span class=3D"cite-bracke=
t">[</span>424<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"ht=
tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sapience" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Sapien=
ce">Sapience</a> (a set of capacities related to high intelligence, such as=
 discernment or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-awareness" ti=
tle=3D"Self-awareness">self-awareness</a>) may provide another moral basis =
for AI rights.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-Thomson-2022_452-1" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-T=
homson-2022-452"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>423<span class=3D"cit=
e-bracket">]</span></a></sup> <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robo=
t_rights" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Robot rights">Robot rights</a> are=
 also sometimes proposed as a practical way to integrate autonomous agents =
into society.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-454" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-454"><span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>425<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup=
>
</p><p>In 2017, the European Union considered granting "electronic personho=
od" to some of the most capable AI systems. Similarly to the legal status o=
f companies, it would have conferred rights but also responsibilities.<sup =
id=3D"cite_ref-455" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-455"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[=
</span>426<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> Critics argued in=
 2018 that granting rights to AI systems would downplay the importance of <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights" title=3D"Human rights=
">human rights</a>, and that legislation should focus on user needs rather =
than speculative futuristic scenarios. They also noted that robots lacked t=
he autonomy to take part in society on their own.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-456" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-456"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>427<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><sup id=3D"cite_ref-457" class=3D"refer=
ence"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite=
_note-457"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>428<span class=3D"cite-brac=
ket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Progress in AI increased interest in the topic. Proponents of AI wel=
fare and rights often argue that AI sentience, if it emerges, would be part=
icularly easy to deny. They warn that this may be a <a href=3D"https://en.w=
ikipedia.org/wiki/Moral_blindness" title=3D"Moral blindness">moral blind sp=
ot</a> analogous to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slavery" title=
=3D"Slavery">slavery</a> or <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factor=
y_farming" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Factory farming">factory farming<=
/a>, which could lead to <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suffering=
_risks" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Suffering risks">large-scale sufferi=
ng</a> if sentient AI is created and carelessly exploited.<sup id=3D"cite_r=
ef-Kateman-2023_453-1" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Kateman-2023-453"><span class=3D=
"cite-bracket">[</span>424<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup><s=
up id=3D"cite_ref-Thomson-2022_452-2" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-Thomson-2022-452"=
><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>423<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</sp=
an></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"Future">Future</h2></div>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Superintelligence_and_the_s=
ingularity">Superintelligence and the singularity</h3></div>
<p>A <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Superintelligence" title=3D"S=
uperintelligence">superintelligence</a> is a hypothetical agent that would =
possess intelligence far surpassing that of the brightest and most gifted h=
uman mind.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERoberts2016_441-1" class=3D"reference=
"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_not=
e-FOOTNOTERoberts2016-441"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>413<span cl=
ass=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> If research into <a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_general_intelligence" title=3D"Artificial=
 general intelligence">artificial general intelligence</a> produced suffici=
ently intelligent software, it might be able to <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Recursive_self-improvement" title=3D"Recursive self-improveme=
nt">reprogram and improve itself</a>. The improved software would be even b=
etter at improving itself, leading to what <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/I._J._Good" title=3D"I. J. Good">I. J. Good</a> called an "<a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligence_explosion" class=3D"mw-redir=
ect" title=3D"Intelligence explosion">intelligence explosion</a>" and <a hr=
ef=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vernor_Vinge" title=3D"Vernor Vinge">Ve=
rnor Vinge</a> called a "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technolog=
ical_singularity" title=3D"Technological singularity">singularity</a>".<sup=
 id=3D"cite_ref-458" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.or=
g/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-458"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">=
[</span>429<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>However, technologies cannot improve exponentially indefinitely, and=
 typically follow an <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S-shaped_curv=
e" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"S-shaped curve">S-shaped curve</a>, slowi=
ng when they reach the physical limits of what the technology can do.<sup i=
d=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTERussellNorvig20211005_459-0" class=3D"reference"><a h=
ref=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOT=
NOTERussellNorvig20211005-459"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>430<spa=
n class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading3"><h3 id=3D"Transhumanism">Transhumanis=
m</h3></div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transhumanism" title=
=3D"Transhumanism">Transhumanism</a></div>
<p>Robot designer <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hans_Moravec" ti=
tle=3D"Hans Moravec">Hans Moravec</a>, cyberneticist <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Kevin_Warwick" title=3D"Kevin Warwick">Kevin Warwick</a>=
 and inventor <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ray_Kurzweil" title=
=3D"Ray Kurzweil">Ray Kurzweil</a> have predicted that humans and machines =
may merge in the future into <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cybor=
g" title=3D"Cyborg">cyborgs</a> that are more capable and powerful than eit=
her. This idea, called transhumanism, has roots in the writings of <a href=
=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldous_Huxley" title=3D"Aldous Huxley">Al=
dous Huxley</a> and <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Ettinge=
r" title=3D"Robert Ettinger">Robert Ettinger</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-460" c=
lass=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_inte=
lligence#cite_note-460"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>431<span class=
=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Fredkin" title=3D"Ed=
ward Fredkin">Edward Fredkin</a> argues that "artificial intelligence is th=
e next step in evolution", an idea first proposed by <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Butler_(novelist)" title=3D"Samuel Butler (noveli=
st)">Samuel Butler</a>'s "<a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darwin_a=
mong_the_Machines" title=3D"Darwin among the Machines">Darwin among the Mac=
hines</a>" as far back as 1863, and expanded upon by <a href=3D"https://en.=
wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Dyson_(science_historian)" title=3D"George Dyson =
(science historian)">George Dyson</a> in his 1998 book <i><a href=3D"https:=
//en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darwin_Among_the_Machines#Evolution_of_Global_Intel=
ligence" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Darwin Among the Machines">Darwin A=
mong the Machines: The Evolution of Global Intelligence</a></i>.<sup id=3D"=
cite_ref-461" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-461"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span=
>432<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p>
<div class=3D"mw-heading mw-heading2"><h2 id=3D"In_fiction">In fiction</h2>=
</div>
<link rel=3D"mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href=3D"mw-data:TemplateStyles:r=
1320445320"><div role=3D"note" class=3D"hatnote navigation-not-searchable">=
Main article: <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellige=
nce_in_fiction" title=3D"Artificial intelligence in fiction">Artificial int=
elligence in fiction</a></div>
<figure class=3D"mw-default-size" typeof=3D"mw:File/Thumb"><a href=3D"https=
://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Capek_play.jpg" class=3D"mw-file-description"=
><img src=3D"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Cape=
k_play.jpg/330px-Capek_play.jpg" decoding=3D"async" width=3D"300" height=3D=
"167" class=3D"mw-file-element" data-file-width=3D"566" data-file-height=3D=
"316"></a><figcaption>The word "robot" itself was coined by <a href=3D"http=
s://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karel_%C4%8Capek" title=3D"Karel =C4=8Capek">Kare=
l =C4=8Capek</a> in his 1921 play <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wi=
ki/R.U.R." title=3D"R.U.R.">R.U.R.</a></i>, the title standing for "Rossum'=
s Universal Robots".</figcaption></figure>
<p>Thought-capable artificial beings have appeared as storytelling devices =
since antiquity,<sup id=3D"cite_ref-AI_in_myth_462-0" class=3D"reference"><=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-A=
I_in_myth-462"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>433<span class=3D"cite-=
bracket">]</span></a></sup> and have been a persistent theme in <a href=3D"=
https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Science_fiction" title=3D"Science fiction">sc=
ience fiction</a>.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcCorduck2004340=E2=80=93400_=
463-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artific=
ial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMcCorduck2004340%E2%80%93400-463"><span =
class=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>434<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a>=
</sup>
</p><p>A common <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trope_(literature)=
" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Trope (literature)">trope</a> in these wor=
ks began with <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Shelley" title=
=3D"Mary Shelley">Mary Shelley</a>'s <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org=
/wiki/Frankenstein" title=3D"Frankenstein">Frankenstein</a></i>, where a hu=
man creation becomes a threat to its masters. This includes such works as <=
a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2001:_A_Space_Odyssey_(novel)" titl=
e=3D"2001: A Space Odyssey (novel)">Arthur C. Clarke's</a> and <a href=3D"h=
ttps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2001:_A_Space_Odyssey" title=3D"2001: A Space =
Odyssey">Stanley Kubrick's</a> <i>2001: A Space Odyssey</i> (both 1968), wi=
th <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HAL_9000" title=3D"HAL 9000">HA=
L 9000</a>, the murderous computer in charge of the <i><a href=3D"https://e=
n.wikipedia.org/wiki/Discovery_One" class=3D"mw-redirect" title=3D"Discover=
y One">Discovery One</a></i> spaceship, as well as <i><a href=3D"https://en=
.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Terminator" title=3D"The Terminator">The Terminator=
</a></i> (1984) and <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Matrix"=
 title=3D"The Matrix">The Matrix</a></i> (1999). In contrast, the rare loya=
l robots such as Gort from <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_=
Day_the_Earth_Stood_Still" title=3D"The Day the Earth Stood Still">The Day =
the Earth Stood Still</a></i> (1951) and Bishop from <i><a href=3D"https://=
en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aliens_(film)" title=3D"Aliens (film)">Aliens</a></i>=
 (1986) are less prominent in popular culture.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEB=
uttazzo2001_464-0" class=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/=
wiki/Artificial_intelligence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEButtazzo2001-464"><span clas=
s=3D"cite-bracket">[</span>435<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></su=
