</p><p><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isaac_Asimov" title=3D"Isaa=
c Asimov">Isaac Asimov</a> introduced the <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.o=
rg/wiki/Three_Laws_of_Robotics" title=3D"Three Laws of Robotics">Three Laws=
 of Robotics</a> in many stories, most notably with the "<a href=3D"https:/=
/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multivac" title=3D"Multivac">Multivac</a>" super-int=
elligent computer. Asimov's laws are often brought up during lay discussion=
s of machine ethics;<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEAnderson2008_465-0" class=
=3D"reference"><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intellig=
ence#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAnderson2008-465"><span class=3D"cite-bracket">[</sp=
an>436<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup> while almost all arti=
ficial intelligence researchers are familiar with Asimov's laws through pop=
ular culture, they generally consider the laws useless for many reasons, on=
e of which is their ambiguity.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOTNOTEMcCauley2007_466-=
