t">[</span>437<span class=3D"cite-bracket">]</span></a></sup>
</p><p>Several works use AI to force us to confront the fundamental questio=
n of what makes us human, showing us artificial beings that have <a href=3D=
"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentience" title=3D"Sentience">the ability t=
o feel</a>, and thus to suffer. This appears in <a href=3D"https://en.wikip=
edia.org/wiki/Karel_%C4%8Capek" title=3D"Karel =C4=8Capek">Karel =C4=8Capek=
</a>'s <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R.U.R." title=3D"R.U.R."=
>R.U.R.</a></i>, the films <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A.I.=
_Artificial_Intelligence" title=3D"A.I. Artificial Intelligence">A.I. Artif=
icial Intelligence</a></i> and <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/=
Ex_Machina_(film)" title=3D"Ex Machina (film)">Ex Machina</a></i>, as well =
as the novel <i><a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Do_Androids_Dream_=
of_Electric_Sheep%3F" title=3D"Do Androids Dream of Electric Sheep?">Do And=
roids Dream of Electric Sheep?</a></i>, by <a href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.=
org/wiki/Philip_K._Dick" title=3D"Philip K. Dick">Philip K. Dick</a>. Dick =
considers the idea that our understanding of human subjectivity is altered =
by technology created with artificial intelligence.<sup id=3D"cite_ref-FOOT=
